WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.680
Welcome, everyone,
I am Felicia Day,

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:04.880
and this is
The Witcher: Unlocked,

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.040
where Netflix Geeked
gives you an exclusive look

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.760
into the shows you love.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.960
Today we're talking Season 2
of The Witcher,

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.920
and what a season
of storms it was.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.320
(grunting, shouting)

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.840
♪

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.720
So, what does destiny
have in store for us?

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.440
Well, we'll get reacquainted
with our Season 1 favorites.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.000
Yeah, you're about to try
and kill my kid.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.680
(laughter)

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.200
What did you call me?

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.960
As well as sitting down with
the new faces of Season 2.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.280
I said to Lambert,
"What the hell was that?"

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:36.120
And he was like...

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.240
"They're basilisks, Coen."

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:38.560
(laughter)

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.560
We're gonna be deep-diving
into the finale

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.640
and discussing, oh yeah,

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:45.560
that ending reveal
with the showrunner herself.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:48.960
That conversation will all
be laid into Blood Origin.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.360
And you're really
getting spoiled today

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.040
'cause we also have
some deleted scenes to show you.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:55.680
-What happened?
-Now, I dare say,

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.200
the time of the White Chill
is nigh.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.880
Before we venture further
into the Continent, be warned.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:03.840
Spoilers abound.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.800
Best tread lightly, hm?

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.280
♪

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:08.160
Let's kick everything off

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.840
by looking at
our heroes' journeys.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.400
We are sitting down
with the cast of The Witcher

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.400
to discuss where
these beloved characters

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.040
ended up in Season 2.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:16.680
Joey Batey,
the one and only,

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:19.720
who plays the beloved
Bard Jaskier.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:20.920
Anya Chalotra, who plays

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:24.320
the masterful sorceress
Yennefer of Vengerberg.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.840
Freya Allan,
also known as Princess Cirilla,

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:28.920
or just Ciri to her friends.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.680
And, of course,
the White Wolf,

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:33.640
our Geralt of Rivia,
Henry Cavill.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:34.520
Hello.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.040
Okay, welcome, Witcher family.

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:38.960
I literally got chills
when I saw you all on screen

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:40.520
together for the first time.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:42.040
Now you're in my chairs.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:43.640
There's chills again.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:46.600
How was it to finally shoot
scenes together?

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:48.360
Henry, let's start with you.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.720
Uh, well,
most of the partnership

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:55.000
was between Freya and I
when it came to the season,

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.040
and enormously enjoyable.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.200
I mean, we had one
very, very brief scene

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:01.640
-in Season 1.
-Yes.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.920
And so, it was nice
to actually get

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:07.440
into the Cirilla/Geralt
relationship.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.680
A long journey
but an enjoyable one.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:11.160
Yeah. Freya?

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.320
Yeah, I was really
looking forward to it,

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:14.880
because we had one scene.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.160
And also, I swear,

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:18.400
like the majority of it
got kind of cut as well

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:19.920
as an add-on.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.880
So, yeah, no,
it was so exciting.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.240
I was really intrigued
to kind of see

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.400
how we were gonna create this
relationship between them,

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.120
because, you know,
it's something

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:30.480
that people
are really waiting to see

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.880
and is then known
for becoming so close

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:35.160
and like a father and daughter.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:38.200
So, yeah, I just couldn't wait
to, like, establish that.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:39.720
Well, you absolutely
accomplished it.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.840
It's a beautiful arc
over the season.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.760
Anya and Joey, you got
to work together as well.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:44.760
How was that?

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:45.880
-Finally.
-Finally.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:46.680
(laughter)

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:47.600
Yeah, we did.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.360
I loved it.
He's a hoot.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:50.360
(laughter)

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:54.360
No, also,
I got to work with Ciri.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:56.640
And, you know, we developed
a trust with each other outside,

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.400
so I was really excited
for this relationship

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.400
because we hadn't, you know,
worked together.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.640
We'd been--we'd, you know,
gotten close in Budapest,

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.200
but we hadn't had that time.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.720
And Eamon as well,
you know.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.560
Yeah, I got a text from Anya,

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.160
I think,
when the scripts landed,

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:11.720
and we were just punching
the air being like,

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:13.960
"We get to act together,
this is so fun."

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:15.200
-Yeah.
-That's wonderful,

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.360
the charisma
between you two is so great.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:18.600
To the White Wolf himself,

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.960
Henry, it is just amazing
to have you here.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:23.400
Let's talk about Geralt's
relationship with Ciri.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.520
It's really the beating heart
of the show.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:29.280
How has this bond changed Geralt
as a character?

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:30.960
I'm not too sure
if it's necessarily

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.240
changed Geralt
as a character.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:37.120
Geralt has an instinctual desire
to protect,

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:40.320
as we see in Season 1
when he's telling Roach

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.600
about the first monster
he killed.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.520
He's talking about protecting
someone against other people.

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:47.600
Throughout his life,

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.920
he has then tried
to fight this instinct,

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:52.600
and, ultimately,
the instinct

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.080
ends up winning
a number of times,

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:56.120
and that ends up getting him
into more trouble.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.760
So this is actually more like
an unlocking for Geralt.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:01.520
She's the key to allow him
to really be

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:03.480
who he has always wanted to be.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:05.920
Freya, you had
an action-packed season.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:07.800
New family,
new Witcher powers,

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:09.920
not so many runs
through the woods.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:11.200
I don't know,
were you disappointed in that?

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:12.200
(laughter)

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.240
No, I was very relieved, yeah.

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:17.800
It was long-awaited
to get to finally actually,

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:19.160
yeah, pick up a sword.

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:20.840
Absolutely,
and you were wonderful.

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:23.760
Anya, Yennefer had a lot of
difficult choices this season,

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:25.720
especially after the loss
of her magic,

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:27.800
which I personally
was very devastated about.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.280
-Yeah, me too.
-Yeah. How did you--

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:30.960
Imagine how Nilfgaard felt.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:31.800
Oh yeah.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:32.600
(laughter)

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:33.400
You should see the other guy.

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:34.880
They were literally devastated.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:36.200
(laughter)

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:38.160
How did you feel
about the choices she made

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:40.800
with regard
to Geralt and Ciri?

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:42.880
Ooh.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:43.920
How do I feel about it?

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.320
I kind of went through
the motions, you know,

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:49.120
with Yennefer,
kind of moment to moment.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:51.880
In hindsight,
she made some rash decisions.

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:56.440
But I think, you know,
she didn't know, you know,

00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:58.840
how to deal with the brutality
of the world without magic,

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:02.720
so, you know, her decision
to sacrifice Ciri

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:05.480
was the right one
in the moment.

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:07.040
And that's the thing
about this show,

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:09.480
is like there's no--
I don't know,

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:11.760
just it's up
to the audience

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:13.520
to kind of figure out
what's right or wrong.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:16.640
And it's the beauty of kind of
exposing so much of a character

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:20.360
is you get to see why
they make a certain decision.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:22.760
Joey, let's talk
all things Jaskier.

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:25.400
We see him with his
fancy new alias this season,

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:26.960
the Sandpiper.

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:28.960
If we include Dandelion
from the book,

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:30.600
so, what,
you have like three names now?

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:32.680
-Yeah.
-Well, what's that about?

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:34.520
(laughter)

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:36.720
Yeah, Lauren,
what's that about?

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:38.280
(overlapping chatter)

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:40.520
I think I'm gonna get
a new nickname per season.

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:42.480
Oh yeah? Okay.
The Knife.

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:43.800
You're not allowed
to pick them.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:45.680
(laughter)

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:48.200
How is it exploring a new side
of your character this season?

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:50.720
He's way more emotionally
vulnerable this season.

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:53.120
I think in Season 1,
I was doing--

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:55.560
we were working so closely
together,

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:57.040
and he had this, like,
boyishness,

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:59.320
like a joy in him,
this kind of light

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:00.840
to a lot of the darkness

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:02.000
that you were just
talking about, Henry.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:04.720
And he often felt like
it was his job

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:05.920
to brighten a room.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:09.760
And we get to see that desire
kind of really tested

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:11.960
in a world that is
incredibly dangerous.

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:13.960
And for the first time
in his life,

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:17.480
he's getting the bejesus
kicked out of him

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:19.960
and he doesn't have his best
friend to come and help him.

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:22.280
And he's willing
to put his life on the line

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:25.280
to--to, you know, finally do
something good with his life,

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:27.000
-do something morally good.
-Yeah.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.360
And that was--that was
the biggest treat for me.

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:31.840
I got to see this textured,
detailed character

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:34.240
that Lauren was so keen
to bring to the show.

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:35.320
Yeah, but that scene

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:37.920
where Geralt comes
to get Jaskier in the jail.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.280
That was so much fun
to play.

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:42.480
Ugh, it was such a good scene.

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:46.280
Freya, we really dig into
Ciri's family tree this season.

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:48.120
Obviously, Ciri doesn't know,

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:50.520
but Freya, I would like
to know your reaction

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:51.560
to the big Emhyr reveal.

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:53.200
Was that a jaw-dropper for you?

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:55.080
-It was.
-Yeah.

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:58.800
Did you know about that reveal
in Season 1 or was that just--

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:00.960
-No, no, I didn't.
-Wow.

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:04.000
To be fair, that gets revealed
in the books right at the end,

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:06.320
and there's no way
that, like,

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:08.560
that--that we couldn't
keep that a secret

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.000
for longer than two seasons.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:11.680
-Absolutely.
-So, I--it had to happen.

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:14.400
We talked a lot
about story and character,

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:17.000
but I would really like
to just touch upon the costumes

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:19.240
because they were
so incredible this season.

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:22.440
And I would love to just hear
how they were to wear.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:24.520
Anya, why don't we start
with you?

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:27.320
Oh, yeah, well, I've got
amazing--amazing costumes.

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:30.040
I mean,
whenever we start a season,

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:30.960
I get to try them on,
and I'm like,

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.040
"Oh, wow,
I can't wait to wear this.

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:34.680
I'm gonna feel so good."

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:37.120
And--and then
they're covered with mud

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:40.920
and they're like probably
like 10 times heavier

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:44.440
because I--I'm probably
in an aqueduct.

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:48.200
So, yeah, they're not as fun
to wear then.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:50.480
I mean, with all the physical
stuff that we have to do

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:54.680
and kind of how flexible
we need to be with our bodies.

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:56.880
And we don't--I don't really
wear corsets as such,

00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:59.280
but I'm kind of sucked in
to my costume,

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:02.000
so they're not as fun
to wear then.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:03.440
But Lucinda,
the costume designer,

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:05.520
you know, made sure
that we did have room

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:08.320
this season to kind of do
everything that we need to do.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:10.400
Everything was, you know,
all the demands of the show.

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:12.240
So, she's--she's brilliant.

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:14.800
Yeah, she was amazing
because she, like,

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:16.600
really allowed me to--

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:18.360
'cause I had kind of
quite a clear vision

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:21.280
of what I wanted her main,
like, Witcher look to be.

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:23.280
So I kind of
wanted her to have

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:25.960
this kind of rough tomboy
kind of feel to it,

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:27.480
like she just got
scraps of clothes

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:28.760
and put them together.

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:31.320
But then, like, she also
wears this kind of corset,

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:35.520
which I think kind of
added a femininity to it.

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:39.080
Which I think it kind of
reflects the story

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:40.960
in terms of Triss coming in

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:43.160
and kind of her trying
to embrace that side of her

00:08:43.160 --> 00:08:46.360
as well as this more
kind of dirty fighter

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:48.240
that she's also become.

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:49.400
So I kind of, yeah,
I don't know,

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:50.600
that was something that
I really enjoyed,

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:51.800
like, creating with her,

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:55.080
and she really allowed me
to, like, be a part of it,

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:56.480
-which was so nice.
-Yeah, that's fantastic,

00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:58.040
you got to kind of develop
that for your character.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:08:59.400
'Cause I think
it's really important, like,

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:01.480
you're wearing them every day,

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:02.920
and so you have
to kind of feel

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:05.400
like it works
for your character.

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:06.720
Yeah, I can imagine
a lot of people

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:08.600
are gonna be cosplaying
that outfit,

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:10.960
because it is feminine
but at the same time tough,

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:13.240
and, yeah,
it's really wonderful.

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:15.480
Henry, let's talk
about Geralt's family.

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:18.400
So, we kind of meet
his chosen family this season.

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:21.520
The Witchers,
Vesemir, Nenneke.

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:23.960
How would you like to explore
this family moving forward?

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:27.800
Is there anyone you particularly
want to spend more time with?

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:30.440
It's--it's a tricky one,
really, because--

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:31.920
and it's also tricky,
I mean,

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:33.120
defining them
as chosen family.

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:34.960
Nenneke, I think, is chosen,

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:39.040
but being left at the doorstep
of Kaer Morhen

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:42.800
and forced to become a Witcher
doesn't feel that chosen.

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:45.160
Okay, okay, technicality.

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:46.280
To be true to the books,

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:49.640
I think there is--
there's a chance to explore

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:52.320
the Nenneke relationship
a little further.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:54.720
Of course, I would love to work
with the Witchers some more,

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:57.280
but it all depends on
how much the story allows.

00:09:57.280 --> 00:09:59.480
I'm a huge fan of the books
and staying loyal to them,

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:03.880
and--and it's--it's about
making sure that story happens

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:06.000
without too much
in the way of diversions

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:09.160
or side things going on
to muddy the waters.

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:12.520
Geralt seems torn between
two worlds this season.

00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:14.800
He's got the Brotherhood
and fatherhood.

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:17.240
How is he balancing his loyalty
to the Witcher brothers

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:19.680
and his role
as Ciri's protector?

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:21.960
Yeah, he was sort of put
in a bit of a tricky spot

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:22.800
with that one.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:24.600
(laughter)

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:28.480
It's--it's definitely
a fine line to tread.

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:30.000
I hope he manages it.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:33.080
I hope we manage to convey it
in a certain way.

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:36.640
But he has made the decision,
as I was saying earlier,

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:41.320
Ciri is this key to unlock this
nature of who he truly is,

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:42.960
and so he's going
to hold onto that.

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:45.640
And as a responsibility,
he will--

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:47.720
he will take care of her,

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:50.800
even if his brothers
want to kill her.

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:52.920
-Yeah.
-Which is quite extreme

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:54.880
-a scenario.
-Yeah.

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:56.400
Well, at least at the end
of this season,

00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:57.280
it turns out well.

00:10:57.280 --> 00:10:58.720
We don't know
about future seasons,

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:01.120
but that--
we'll get to that later.

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:02.240
And I'm hearing all this
and I'm like,

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:03.760
"Sorry? What?

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:05.200
What's going on?

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:06.520
Who wants to kill me?

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:07.440
Not another person."

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:09.240
(laughter)

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:12.040
Joey, we get a sneaky reveal
at the end of the season

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:13.800
when we realize Dijkstra

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:16.080
has been your benefactor
the whole time.

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:18.800
I am really hyped at the thought
of you sharing the screen

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:20.840
with Graham McTavish
and Cassie Clare,

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:22.600
they are fabulous.

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:25.160
Do you see trouble
for Jaskier in Season 3?

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:27.680
I don't think we can--
I think we have to assume

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:31.200
he's gonna be
fairly constantly in trouble,

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:33.960
no matter what and no matter
who he's hanging out with.

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:36.960
But the idea of working
with such amazing actors,

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:39.240
I mean, I'm--
I count myself so lucky

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:40.520
that I get to turn up
every single day

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:42.400
and work with
amazing actors anyway.

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:43.680
And this family,
this Witcher family

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:45.560
is growing ever bigger
and ever stronger,

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:49.560
and so, yeah, I love turning up
and seeing what's coming.

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:53.160
And I'm pretty sure
that--that I'm gonna have

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:55.240
to really work quite hard
to hold my own

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:57.840
if someone like Graham
is gonna be acting opposite me.

00:11:57.840 --> 00:11:59.760
I'd probably
just forget my lines

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:02.520
and sit in marveled awe
at this man.

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:05.520
But, yeah,
I'm excited to see what comes.

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:06.480
You are brilliant.

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:07.640
You'll be perfectly matched.

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:09.120
Oh, see, he looks after me.

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:10.360
-Don't you worry about that.
-Yeah.

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:12.240
And you're lovably disheveled,
you know,

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:13.440
like that's a lovable thing
about you.

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:14.480
Lovably disheveled.

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:15.560
(laughter)

00:12:15.560 --> 00:12:17.760
Can we--I'm gonna
put that on my CV.

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:19.240
(laughter)

00:12:19.240 --> 00:12:21.440
That's another t-shirt idea
right there.

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:22.520
(laughter)

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:25.480
Anya, Yennefer had
a really tough time this season.

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:27.880
She had to hang out
with Cahir a lot.

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:31.040
That must have been kind of
awkward, character-wise.

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:33.480
The show really lives
in this wonderful gray area,

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:35.480
so do you think
the alliances

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:37.400
with some of the antagonists
of the series

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:38.640
have kind of changed
what she thinks

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:40.040
about good and evil?

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:42.640
Ooh, good question.

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:47.560
Hm, yeah, I think putting
these two characters together

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:49.160
was a very exciting choice.

00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:51.560
They've really challenged
each other in so many ways.

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:53.560
For Yennefer, her perspective
definitely changes

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:54.560
through this season.

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:57.200
She matures a lot and,
you know,

00:12:57.200 --> 00:12:59.440
definitely becomes
less selfish,

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:01.720
because the situations
she finds herself in,

00:13:01.720 --> 00:13:03.640
kind of she's had
to consider things more,

00:13:03.640 --> 00:13:08.000
like she's less kind of
rational without magic.

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:13.560
And, yeah, she comes
to a more selfless place

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:15.240
at the end of this
because of her--

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:16.840
the new relationships
in this season.

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:18.560
Yeah, it's a beautiful arc.

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:20.240
Now, we're on Netflix Geeked,

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:24.040
so let's talk about what you
guys personally geek out about.

00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:25.840
Henry, let's start with you.

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:27.600
Specifically whicher universe
or just...

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:28.800
-I mean, just anything.
-Oh, my goodness.

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:30.600
How long have you got?

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:32.480
You've got so many and I've got none.

00:13:32.480 --> 00:13:34.800
Yeah, I was like, how much time do we have?

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:36.680
Listen, I'll sit here
all day listening.

00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:38.560
-Ruling out The Witcher.
-Yeah, not The Witcher.

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:42.240
I mean, the easy answer
is Warhammer 40,000.

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:45.080
That is my--my jam.

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:47.320
I have been into it
since I was 10.

00:13:47.320 --> 00:13:48.920
What's your army at the moment?

00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:50.400
-Custodes.
-Ah.

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:52.520
I've--I'm going--I'm going
Necrons next time.

00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:54.640
Necrons? Okay.
All right. Cool.

00:13:54.640 --> 00:13:56.920
Were you into--
were you into this before

00:13:56.920 --> 00:13:57.880
or is this a new thing?

00:13:57.880 --> 00:13:59.840
Let's just assume
that anything nerdy

00:13:59.840 --> 00:14:01.200
I've been doing
for a good 33 years.

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:02.520
Oh, wow, we can talk for hours.

00:14:02.520 --> 00:14:04.920
Yeah, so, yeah, this is--
that's my jam

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:07.280
and that's the stuff I do
in my free time.

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:09.000
That's amazing, that's amazing.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:11.040
I'd love to see your armies,
your collection.

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:12.480
-Do you paint them yourself?
-I do, yes.

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:13.800
-That is so cool.
-Yeah.

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:14.720
Is it?

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:15.840
(laughter)

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:17.520
It's cool when you--

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:18.960
Cool in a certain world, yeah.

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:19.920
I like painting,
there you go,

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:21.080
so I think
I'd really enjoy that.

00:14:21.080 --> 00:14:21.960
It's so therapeutic.

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:23.080
I think--I think
you'd be surprised.

00:14:23.080 --> 00:14:24.520
Yeah, it's actually
really satisfying.

00:14:24.520 --> 00:14:26.000
So is that your geeky thing,
is painting?

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:27.800
It could be,
Henry might get me onto it.

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:29.120
Otherwise, it's--

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:30.840
oh no, my geeky thing
is probably cooking--

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:32.240
like watching cooking videos.

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:33.320
No, no, no, that's wonderful.

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:35.240
Listen, I like watching people
renovate houses.

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:36.520
I don't care, I'm shameless.

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:38.560
I love cooking as well,
so that's probably why.

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:39.760
Anything about cooking.

00:14:39.760 --> 00:14:41.520
But the painting thing,
you could bring some--

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:43.680
this is what you need to do
in your trailers

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:45.240
on the off-time
during Season 3,

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:46.240
you paint minis and you just--

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:47.880
-We don't have off-time.
-What's off-time?

00:14:47.880 --> 00:14:48.840
(laughter)

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:50.000
Off-time? Yeah, yeah, yeah,
off-time?

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:52.400
(overlapping chatter)

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:56.400
Yeah, can we bake that
into the schedule? Off-time.

00:14:56.400 --> 00:14:58.960
It'll be like lunch hour
and then painting time.

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:01.000
-Yeah. Yeah.
-Right on the call sheet.

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:03.680
Henry's painting time.

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:04.920
Freya, are you gonna
join them in to paint

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:05.880
or do you have another--

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:08.120
Oh, I love painting,
yeah, yeah.

00:15:08.120 --> 00:15:11.320
Art is like my--no, I actually
genuinely love art, yeah.

00:15:11.320 --> 00:15:13.680
I do--I do geek out
about that.

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:15.280
-Yeah.
-I was gonna say Harry Potter,

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:17.320
but, yeah, art sounds cooler.

00:15:17.320 --> 00:15:19.600
I mean, sorry,
Harry Potter's great.

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:21.040
Harry Potter's--I'm--yeah.

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:22.240
-Well, yeah.
-I don't know,

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:23.240
-there's a bunch of people.
-I mean, it's different

00:15:23.240 --> 00:15:24.200
kind of realm, isn't it?

00:15:24.200 --> 00:15:26.000
But I'm gonna be
definitely watching

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:27.280
all of them at Christmas.

00:15:27.280 --> 00:15:28.680
I know, it's a--it's a good one.

00:15:28.680 --> 00:15:30.360
You have
a Luna Lovegood quality,

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:32.360
and she's my favorite character,
so, okay.

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:35.160
Okay, Witcher family,
reading glasses on,

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:37.800
because we are gonna
talk about the books,

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:39.480
the source material
behind the show,

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:41.960
Andrzej Sapkowski's
epic stories.

00:15:41.960 --> 00:15:44.080
Bookworms will know
that Blood of Elves

00:15:44.080 --> 00:15:45.720
is the season's main reference,

00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:47.640
but we actually open
with a short story,

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:49.280
A Grain of Truth.

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:51.240
Henry, you've been a fan
of the Witcher universe

00:15:51.240 --> 00:15:53.280
-for a long time.
-Mm-hm.

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:55.360
Was this a story
you were excited to see?

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:59.120
Yeah, from the books,
I really loved that story.

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:03.440
It was a story
of perspectives

00:16:03.440 --> 00:16:06.960
and forced scenarios

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:10.360
and senses of guilt
and redemption.

00:16:10.360 --> 00:16:12.880
And it's obviously
a subverted version

00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:15.000
of Beauty and the Beast,

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:16.840
but done in such a masterful way
in the books.

00:16:16.840 --> 00:16:19.200
There's so much nuance
and so much complexity.

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:20.960
And the answers
are never quite

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:22.240
what you think
they're going to be,

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:23.880
and your hero
doesn't necessarily make

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:25.600
the choices
that you would make,

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:29.640
but they are still
potentially ethical

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:30.640
and moral choices.

00:16:30.640 --> 00:16:31.840
And it's always up
for discussion.

00:16:31.840 --> 00:16:33.880
That's why I say it's always
about perspective.

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:35.960
And that's what I loved about it
in the books so much.

00:16:35.960 --> 00:16:37.960
It's an extraordinary piece
of writing.

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:40.560
And what was it like to film
along Kristofer Hivju?

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:43.200
-'Cause he was with the--
-Absolutely amazing.

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:44.440
I love that man.

00:16:44.440 --> 00:16:47.840
He is--I mean, aside from being
such an extraordinary energy,

00:16:47.840 --> 00:16:51.880
he is--he is just beautiful
and an amazing talent.

00:16:51.880 --> 00:16:53.280
He was playing Nivellen

00:16:53.280 --> 00:16:55.840
in such a large character
kind of way,

00:16:55.840 --> 00:16:58.960
but then right at the end
when the curse is lifted,

00:16:58.960 --> 00:17:01.600
he busts out
this Shakespearean performance,

00:17:01.600 --> 00:17:03.800
and it is beautiful.

00:17:03.800 --> 00:17:05.040
And it just--it just--

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:07.080
it shows the quality
of that actor,

00:17:07.080 --> 00:17:09.760
that he can embrace
this larger-than-life,

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:11.720
kind of fun character,

00:17:11.720 --> 00:17:13.440
and then deep down
he's still got

00:17:13.440 --> 00:17:16.240
this extraordinary
soulful performance as well,

00:17:16.240 --> 00:17:18.400
which he's not trying
to force upon anyone.

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:20.640
He's not saying, "No, no, no,
but wait, wait, I can do this."

00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:22.040
He's just waiting
for his opportunity,

00:17:22.040 --> 00:17:24.480
and then he's like, "And here
you go, ladies and gents."

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:26.320
And it's--
he's an extraordinary man.

00:17:26.320 --> 00:17:29.120
And kind and intelligent
and lovely to spend time with.

00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:30.560
Yeah, it was so funny

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:32.680
shooting this scene
at the table,

00:17:32.680 --> 00:17:33.960
because it was just--
just us three,

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:36.880
and we've just got food
just flying out of the sky.

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:39.200
And you never know
how it's gonna land,

00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:40.880
so you can imagine
that that was like--

00:17:40.880 --> 00:17:42.720
like just completely absurd,
really.

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:44.120
-Food everywhere.
-Yeah.

00:17:44.120 --> 00:17:46.200
Your reactions were so funny
but so serious.

00:17:46.200 --> 00:17:48.240
It made you really love
the character

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:50.160
that you've seen running in
the woods for a whole season.

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:51.840
And you're like, "Oh, she's
really funny and charming."

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:54.480
And it just really humanized
Ciri in a wonderful way.

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:55.640
Yeah, it was really great.

00:17:55.640 --> 00:17:58.120
Freya, Ciri and Geralt
often spent more time apart

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:00.680
than together,
in the books and in the series.

00:18:00.680 --> 00:18:03.160
Was there a particular scene
that you really loved

00:18:03.160 --> 00:18:04.960
Ciri and Geralt
being together for?

00:18:04.960 --> 00:18:08.360
Oh, yep, it was one
that actually you said

00:18:08.360 --> 00:18:09.520
should be put in, right,

00:18:09.520 --> 00:18:11.920
the one in episode one
where we're by the campfire

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:12.720
-at the end.
-Yep.

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:14.200
Yeah, so that
was Henry's idea

00:18:14.200 --> 00:18:15.600
to like have
something there,

00:18:15.600 --> 00:18:17.120
which I think
was such a good idea.

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:19.360
And, like, honestly,
that was--that was one of my--

00:18:19.360 --> 00:18:21.000
I don't know,
it was something--

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:22.400
something about it
was like we--

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:23.400
I don't know,
I don't know what it was,

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:24.400
we just connected.

00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:26.400
I mean, your performance
is brilliant in that,

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:27.480
-it's absolutely stunning.
-Thanks.

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:30.760
But I think we ad-libbed
most of that scene.

00:18:30.760 --> 00:18:33.000
They ended up cutting it
as scripted anyway,

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:35.680
but there was genuine feeling
there.

00:18:35.680 --> 00:18:38.880
We were talking to one another
as performers in character,

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:40.280
and there was real feeling.

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:41.920
It was Geralt
telling the story of--

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:45.320
he was being a paternal
or fraternal figure

00:18:45.320 --> 00:18:48.880
in such a way
that Ciri felt safe,

00:18:48.880 --> 00:18:50.440
despite the fact that
they had just gone through

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:52.880
this horrible thing where
terrible mistakes had been made

00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:54.600
and it could have gone
so horribly wrong,

00:18:54.600 --> 00:18:56.400
-but it felt honest and real.
-Wait, which--

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:57.640
what campfire scene
are you talking about?

00:18:57.640 --> 00:18:59.040
Because I think I'm talking
about a different one.

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:00.160
The one right at the end
of episode one.

00:19:00.160 --> 00:19:00.960
Oh, yeah.
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:02.360
-Same one.
-Oh, right. Sorry.

00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:03.720
Clearly remembered it
very differently.

00:19:03.720 --> 00:19:04.880
So, there's two--yeah.

00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:07.080
(laughter)

00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:08.040
It all blends together.

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:09.120
No, something you said
made it sound like

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:10.240
it was the one before.

00:19:10.240 --> 00:19:11.560
-Sorry.
-No, it's the same.

00:19:11.560 --> 00:19:13.080
We are talking
about the same campfire.

00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:17.880
You guys, you all win Emmys
around every campfire.

00:19:17.880 --> 00:19:20.280
-So many campfires.
-Yeah, so many campfires.

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:21.560
Some of them reshot
as well, so...

00:19:21.560 --> 00:19:23.600
Yeah, exactly,
I've gotten lost in them all.

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:25.160
Now, if you know the books,

00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:27.040
chances are you know
the "Dear Friend" letters

00:19:27.040 --> 00:19:28.520
between Yennefer and Geralt.

00:19:28.520 --> 00:19:31.040
They are a moment of comedy
that really sums up

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:33.360
all the dysfunction
of the relationship.

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:36.120
They're very funny
but not very romantic.

00:19:36.120 --> 00:19:37.560
We don't see the letters
in Season 2,

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:39.640
but we do get a nice nod
toward them.

00:19:39.640 --> 00:19:42.560
Let us take a look.

00:19:42.560 --> 00:19:44.200
Geralt, do you know
what to do with this?

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:45.480
-Sorry.
-Ciri.

00:19:45.480 --> 00:19:46.480
Sorry.

00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:47.360
This is my...

00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:48.840
You're so good in this.

00:19:48.840 --> 00:19:51.560
...dear friend.

00:19:51.560 --> 00:19:53.400
"Dear friend."

00:19:56.720 --> 00:19:58.680
This is Yennefer.

00:19:59.640 --> 00:20:01.680
You're Yennefer.

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:04.000
And you must be his...

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:05.440
Child Surprise.

00:20:05.440 --> 00:20:09.160
♪

00:20:09.160 --> 00:20:10.360
Oh, you're so mean.

00:20:10.360 --> 00:20:12.320
Yeah, to be fair, you don't
really have much right

00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:14.360
-to be pissed off.
-Exactly.

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:16.600
You're--yeah, you're about
to try and kill my kid.

00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:18.320
(laughter)

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:19.840
What did you call me?

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:22.160
(laughter)

00:20:22.160 --> 00:20:24.240
Anya, Henry, and Freya,
in the books,

00:20:24.240 --> 00:20:26.640
your characters have this
very tricky relationship,

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:28.600
as we saw,
and in the show it's the same.

00:20:28.600 --> 00:20:30.520
Anya, you want
to sacrifice his kid,

00:20:30.520 --> 00:20:32.440
you hold a knife
to her throat.

00:20:32.440 --> 00:20:34.520
Like, how do you think
they feel about--

00:20:34.520 --> 00:20:36.360
-Happy family!
-Exactly.

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:37.760
-Yeah.
-Saturday night in my house.

00:20:37.760 --> 00:20:39.800
(laughter)

00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:41.280
It's just like Monopoly, right?

00:20:41.280 --> 00:20:42.800
Yeah, exactly.

00:20:42.800 --> 00:20:44.960
How do you feel, like,
they feel about each other

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:46.320
at the end of the season?

00:20:47.920 --> 00:20:49.440
(overlapping chatter)

00:20:49.440 --> 00:20:50.360
-It's--I mean--
-I don't know.

00:20:50.360 --> 00:20:52.360
-It's tricky.
-It's a tricky one.

00:20:52.360 --> 00:20:55.680
I mean, it's a fairly extreme
set of circumstances

00:20:55.680 --> 00:20:58.360
to come back from.

00:20:58.360 --> 00:21:00.240
I think we're--yeah,

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:01.560
we're gonna have
to play with that

00:21:01.560 --> 00:21:02.880
-a little bit.
-I think we'll have

00:21:02.880 --> 00:21:04.360
to figure that out
in Season 3, to be honest.

00:21:04.360 --> 00:21:07.160
It's--I mean,
it's pretty extreme,

00:21:07.160 --> 00:21:09.600
so it's a tough one
to forgive.

00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:11.560
It's gonna take
some real work

00:21:11.560 --> 00:21:14.240
and some nuance
and some complexity...

00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:16.040
All right, okay,
I've gotta work.

00:21:16.040 --> 00:21:17.520
(laughter)

00:21:17.520 --> 00:21:18.960
Yeah. So, Anya,

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:20.360
where do you see
your character going

00:21:20.360 --> 00:21:21.760
in Season 3 and beyond?

00:21:21.760 --> 00:21:23.080
It's habitual, isn't it?

00:21:23.080 --> 00:21:24.360
She's how old,

00:21:24.360 --> 00:21:26.120
and she's lived
the kind of life she's lived,

00:21:26.120 --> 00:21:28.200
and, you know, the rules
that she lives by,

00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:29.400
and that's the end,

00:21:29.400 --> 00:21:30.800
and she doesn't trust nobody,

00:21:30.800 --> 00:21:33.840
and it's gonna be hard
to just delve into

00:21:33.840 --> 00:21:35.800
a new way of being.

00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:37.520
There are certain--

00:21:37.520 --> 00:21:40.440
she might have learned
to consider things more,

00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:41.520
but in the moment,

00:21:41.520 --> 00:21:44.160
in such a kind of
violent world,

00:21:44.160 --> 00:21:46.080
you know,
you've kind of just gotta

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:47.720
look out for yourself as well.

00:21:47.720 --> 00:21:49.400
So, I don't know,
it's gonna be difficult.

00:21:49.400 --> 00:21:53.240
I think whatever happens with
the storytelling in Season 3,

00:21:53.240 --> 00:21:54.680
Anya's gonna knock it
out of the park.

00:21:54.680 --> 00:21:58.920
She is extraordinary with
what she does with Yennefer.

00:21:58.920 --> 00:22:02.200
The subtlety in her performance
is mind-blowing.

00:22:02.200 --> 00:22:05.520
Like, you are top-notch.

00:22:05.520 --> 00:22:08.280
Jaskier and Yennefer get
more screen time this season,

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:10.040
another great dynamic
from the books.

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:12.960
What's your favorite thing
about working alongside Anya?

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:14.880
Well, I mean,
to echo your thoughts,

00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:17.440
I mean, I think there was
various different moments

00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:20.480
in one of our scenes
where I forgot my lines,

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:22.840
which is not, um--
which is pretty rare for me,

00:22:22.840 --> 00:22:26.480
because if I forget my lines,
which is--I never do,

00:22:26.480 --> 00:22:28.000
but I tend to ad lib anyway

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:29.600
and improvise
and find my way back.

00:22:29.600 --> 00:22:32.200
But I was just in awe
of your performance,

00:22:32.200 --> 00:22:35.560
and so, I would quite often
be so wrapped up

00:22:35.560 --> 00:22:38.520
in the way
that Anya's stillness

00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:42.120
and often in the silences
can do so much

00:22:42.120 --> 00:22:44.200
that I was just enraptured,

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:46.000
that I then went,
"Oh, crap, it's me,

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:48.040
it's my line,
I'm supposed to talk now."

00:22:48.040 --> 00:22:49.880
And there was
a couple of moments like that

00:22:49.880 --> 00:22:51.800
where it made me feel--

00:22:51.800 --> 00:22:53.520
it reminded me
how grateful I am to be

00:22:53.520 --> 00:22:55.400
working with just
such amazing people,

00:22:55.400 --> 00:22:57.720
because, you know,

00:22:57.720 --> 00:22:59.680
that's why you get up
in the morning, really.

00:22:59.680 --> 00:23:01.160
And we got to have some--

00:23:01.160 --> 00:23:03.440
she's the only one
I can't make laugh on set.

00:23:03.440 --> 00:23:04.200
It's gotta be said.

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:06.480
Henry corpses, drop of a hat.

00:23:06.480 --> 00:23:08.000
-Yeah.
-Really?

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:10.040
And will get his revenge on me
and will make me corpse,

00:23:10.040 --> 00:23:11.600
but Anya,
when she's Yennefer,

00:23:11.600 --> 00:23:13.600
she's Yennefer,
and she's unbreakable,

00:23:13.600 --> 00:23:15.720
she's indomitable,
and that's--

00:23:15.720 --> 00:23:18.000
so, Season 3,
that's my objective.

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:20.120
I'm gonna try
and get you to break.

00:23:20.120 --> 00:23:21.600
Gonna learn how to juggle.

00:23:21.600 --> 00:23:23.440
Exactly!

00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:25.240
Yeah, it's a dream,
it really is,

00:23:25.240 --> 00:23:27.840
and I'm looking forward
to Season 3

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:30.320
and seeing how
all of these, like--

00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:32.240
this is the first time
Ciri and Jaskier have met.

00:23:32.240 --> 00:23:36.080
I mean, two best pals
have just made up, and, yeah,

00:23:36.080 --> 00:23:39.000
where does weird Uncle Jaskier
fit into this dynamic

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:41.200
that you strange people
have got going on?

00:23:41.200 --> 00:23:42.560
It's gonna be fun.

00:23:42.560 --> 00:23:43.520
I'm sure you're gonna
make me corpse.

00:23:43.520 --> 00:23:44.920
(laughter)

00:23:44.920 --> 00:23:46.200
-For sure.
-Thank you for answering

00:23:46.200 --> 00:23:47.480
all of my nosey questions.

00:23:47.480 --> 00:23:49.440
Now let's treat the fans
to a deleted scene.

00:23:49.440 --> 00:23:52.000
-Ooh.
-Yeah.

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:53.120
Ciri?

00:23:53.120 --> 00:23:55.560
♪

00:23:55.560 --> 00:23:57.760
-Geralt.
-What happened?

00:23:57.760 --> 00:23:59.360
I saw it.

00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:02.080
♪

00:24:02.080 --> 00:24:04.880
-Ciri.
-Where is she?

00:24:04.880 --> 00:24:06.400
You can't protect her.

00:24:06.400 --> 00:24:08.200
Just tell me
where she is!

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:10.520
She'll destroy us all.

00:24:10.520 --> 00:24:14.480
I saw it.

00:24:14.480 --> 00:24:16.720
You can't stop it.

00:24:16.720 --> 00:24:18.800
You can't stop it!

00:24:22.680 --> 00:24:25.360
Anna's acting you
off the screen there, Henry.

00:24:25.360 --> 00:24:27.880
-Yeah, yeah.
-She's so good!

00:24:27.880 --> 00:24:28.960
Yeah, that really
spooked me out.

00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:29.920
Why was that deleted?

00:24:29.920 --> 00:24:31.760
I don't know, I couldn't--

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:33.640
So many stories to tell.

00:24:33.640 --> 00:24:36.400
To treat the Netflix Geeked
audience--you guys.

00:24:36.400 --> 00:24:37.800
As promised,
that was the exclusive,

00:24:37.800 --> 00:24:40.320
never-before-seen clip
from Season 2.

00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:43.520
It showed us the aftermath
of Triss and Ciri's Dol Dusza,

00:24:43.520 --> 00:24:46.360
a spell intended
to reveal Ciri's power

00:24:46.360 --> 00:24:48.640
that doesn't quite go
as planned.

00:24:48.640 --> 00:24:50.960
Um, Freya, what did you do
to Anna Shaffer,

00:24:50.960 --> 00:24:52.440
'cause that seemed
very dramatic.

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:56.200
Uh, yes, um, well...

00:24:56.200 --> 00:25:00.320
she was strangled,
and I did watch,

00:25:00.320 --> 00:25:05.080
but I was shouting for Geralt
to come and help out.

00:25:05.080 --> 00:25:06.120
So I tried.

00:25:06.120 --> 00:25:07.640
-Yeah. So it's his fault.
-Yes.

00:25:07.640 --> 00:25:09.600
Yes, where were you?

00:25:09.600 --> 00:25:11.120
Always my fault.

00:25:11.120 --> 00:25:12.320
Henry, it's been great
to see more of

00:25:12.320 --> 00:25:15.000
Geralt's relationship
with Triss in Season 2.

00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:16.720
Although this scene
didn't make the final cut,

00:25:16.720 --> 00:25:20.280
I imagine seeing Triss so upset
had an impact on Geralt.

00:25:20.280 --> 00:25:23.320
How do you think he views
Ciri's very scary power

00:25:23.320 --> 00:25:25.240
by the end of the season?

00:25:25.240 --> 00:25:28.560
Um...well, I think
he's always known

00:25:28.560 --> 00:25:30.320
there's something special
about her.

00:25:30.320 --> 00:25:31.720
From the very beginning
of the season,

00:25:31.720 --> 00:25:34.480
if she can see the future,

00:25:34.480 --> 00:25:38.920
then there's something
which isn't just standard.

00:25:38.920 --> 00:25:40.840
She's not the standard
young lady.

00:25:40.840 --> 00:25:44.720
And so, I think it's a matter
of just working out how--

00:25:44.720 --> 00:25:46.560
how great a power that is.

00:25:46.560 --> 00:25:49.520
And the true development
of Geralt this season

00:25:49.520 --> 00:25:53.800
is him being able to recognize
that he can't do this himself.

00:25:53.800 --> 00:25:55.680
He requires outside help,

00:25:55.680 --> 00:25:58.360
and that's a wonderful
reflection from the books,

00:25:58.360 --> 00:26:03.240
when he wrote to Yennefer
the "Dear Friend" letter.

00:26:03.240 --> 00:26:04.680
That is where Geralt grows.

00:26:04.680 --> 00:26:06.120
He becomes this idea
of going,

00:26:06.120 --> 00:26:08.200
"Sh--" uh, don't say that word.

00:26:08.200 --> 00:26:10.280
Um, "Darn!

00:26:10.280 --> 00:26:11.800
I can't do this myself.

00:26:11.800 --> 00:26:16.640
And so, I need to call upon
all of my friends who can help."

00:26:16.640 --> 00:26:18.280
Before I say goodbye
to all of you,

00:26:18.280 --> 00:26:19.880
one final question:

00:26:19.880 --> 00:26:23.360
favorite moment filming
Season 2, and why.

00:26:23.360 --> 00:26:24.960
Let's start with you, Joey.

00:26:24.960 --> 00:26:27.640
Favorite moment, crumbs!

00:26:27.640 --> 00:26:29.880
Um, I think it was
probably actually--

00:26:29.880 --> 00:26:32.360
what sticks in my mind
is my first day of shooting

00:26:32.360 --> 00:26:33.720
after the pandemic.

00:26:33.720 --> 00:26:35.880
Just coming back,
and I was--

00:26:35.880 --> 00:26:38.480
we were halfway up a mountain
in the Lake District,

00:26:38.480 --> 00:26:40.200
and it was just
the first time that--

00:26:40.200 --> 00:26:41.720
I guess you guys had done
a bit of shooting

00:26:41.720 --> 00:26:43.120
before the hiatus,

00:26:43.120 --> 00:26:45.080
and then I was sitting
around in my room

00:26:45.080 --> 00:26:46.640
for six, eight months,
and I don't know,

00:26:46.640 --> 00:26:48.040
it was a really--

00:26:48.040 --> 00:26:49.560
it was a complicated scene,

00:26:49.560 --> 00:26:51.320
and there was
an awful lot of dialogue,

00:26:51.320 --> 00:26:53.400
and I was just
freezing my pants off

00:26:53.400 --> 00:26:57.480
in a lake in the middle
of January, whenever it was.

00:26:57.480 --> 00:26:59.440
Looking ripped!

00:26:59.440 --> 00:27:01.800
Is that all you did,
sit-ups the whole time?

00:27:01.800 --> 00:27:02.800
I just didn't eat.

00:27:02.800 --> 00:27:04.360
(laughter)

00:27:04.360 --> 00:27:07.120
It's not that hard, uh...

00:27:07.120 --> 00:27:08.720
But yeah, that was
really, really fun,

00:27:08.720 --> 00:27:10.800
'cause it was the first time
I'd seen you in a while,

00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:13.240
and it just felt like
coming home again, you know?

00:27:13.240 --> 00:27:14.600
Just all the pals,

00:27:14.600 --> 00:27:17.240
all your friends and colleagues
that you've not seen in so long.

00:27:17.240 --> 00:27:19.160
It just--yeah, that stayed
with me for a while.

00:27:19.160 --> 00:27:21.080
It was so wonderful.

00:27:21.080 --> 00:27:22.760
Anya, what was your
favorite moment?

00:27:22.760 --> 00:27:24.800
Um, my favorite moment was
probably the aqueducts.

00:27:24.800 --> 00:27:26.680
I loved working with
the team that I did--

00:27:26.680 --> 00:27:30.320
Eamon, Kevin, Jamal,
it was a really special scene.

00:27:30.320 --> 00:27:32.720
-Freya?
-Um, I would just say

00:27:32.720 --> 00:27:37.080
all the times when it was
me with all the Witchers.

00:27:37.080 --> 00:27:39.800
Like, I just--I just found
that dynamic so funny.

00:27:39.800 --> 00:27:43.480
Like, me amongst all these,
like, 6-foot-2 men.

00:27:43.480 --> 00:27:45.080
(laughter)

00:27:45.080 --> 00:27:46.840
Just, like, little me
in a white dress.

00:27:46.840 --> 00:27:48.280
There's just, like,
something about it

00:27:48.280 --> 00:27:50.080
which was hysterical,
and so,

00:27:50.080 --> 00:27:51.520
I felt like
I could just tease them all,

00:27:51.520 --> 00:27:54.080
'cause I was in that sort of--
I don't know.

00:27:54.080 --> 00:27:55.080
They were like
my older brothers,

00:27:55.080 --> 00:27:56.680
all of them.

00:27:56.680 --> 00:27:58.320
Yeah. And it really called back
to that Knucklebones scene

00:27:58.320 --> 00:28:00.560
we see, like, in the very,
very beginning of Season 1.

00:28:00.560 --> 00:28:02.960
You really have come
your tomboy way.

00:28:02.960 --> 00:28:04.440
Yeah, exactly.

00:28:04.440 --> 00:28:06.200
And Henry,
what was your favorite scene?

00:28:06.200 --> 00:28:08.440
Um, it's--I mean,
tough to say favorite scene,

00:28:08.440 --> 00:28:09.440
to be honest.

00:28:09.440 --> 00:28:10.720
Tough to say favorite moment.

00:28:10.720 --> 00:28:14.600
But I--I think
my favorite moment--

00:28:14.600 --> 00:28:17.440
or my favorite time was
during the Lake District.

00:28:17.440 --> 00:28:20.800
It was so stunning up there.

00:28:20.800 --> 00:28:22.640
It just felt like a hobbit
was gonna walk around the corner

00:28:22.640 --> 00:28:24.280
every five seconds.

00:28:24.280 --> 00:28:26.480
And--in a good way.

00:28:26.480 --> 00:28:30.360
(laughter)

00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:32.320
I loved it up there.

00:28:32.320 --> 00:28:33.520
I love location work,

00:28:33.520 --> 00:28:36.000
and I feel you can really draw
from the environment around you,

00:28:36.000 --> 00:28:37.800
and we're in
a fantasy show like this,

00:28:37.800 --> 00:28:39.920
and you can really
transport yourself there,

00:28:39.920 --> 00:28:41.760
especially if
you're looking that way

00:28:41.760 --> 00:28:43.640
and all you have is
another character in costume

00:28:43.640 --> 00:28:45.280
and you can't see
the camera crew or anything,

00:28:45.280 --> 00:28:47.000
and you're saying,
"I'm here.

00:28:47.000 --> 00:28:48.880
I'm existing within it."

00:28:48.880 --> 00:28:50.560
And that stuff is extraordinary.

00:28:50.560 --> 00:28:52.520
Thank you so much
for joining me today.

00:28:52.520 --> 00:28:54.240
I feel a lot more magical

00:28:54.240 --> 00:28:56.120
having spent time
with all of you.

00:28:56.120 --> 00:28:58.080
Next, I have three strapping
Witchers joining me,

00:28:58.080 --> 00:29:00.560
so I'm going to need some room
on my chairs.

00:29:00.560 --> 00:29:02.520
♪

00:29:02.520 --> 00:29:03.640
(blade clangs)

00:29:03.640 --> 00:29:07.640
♪

00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:09.040
(coughs)

00:29:09.040 --> 00:29:13.280
♪

00:29:13.280 --> 00:29:14.480
Hello, Witchers.

00:29:14.480 --> 00:29:15.840
Thank you for being here.

00:29:15.840 --> 00:29:17.280
Let's give you all
a proper introduction.

00:29:17.280 --> 00:29:18.480
Yasen Atour,

00:29:18.480 --> 00:29:21.440
who plays Geralt's
Witcher brother in arms, Coen.

00:29:21.440 --> 00:29:22.760
-Thank you.
-Paul Bullion,

00:29:22.760 --> 00:29:24.840
who plays the gruff
but lovable Lambert,

00:29:24.840 --> 00:29:27.360
known to his friends
as Lambchop.

00:29:27.360 --> 00:29:29.280
The wisest of Witchers,
Vesemir,

00:29:29.280 --> 00:29:31.480
is played by the brilliant
Kim Bodnia,

00:29:31.480 --> 00:29:34.400
and of course,
she's a true Witcher at heart,

00:29:34.400 --> 00:29:37.000
our showrunner,
Lauren Schmidt Hissrich.

00:29:37.000 --> 00:29:38.520
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:29:38.520 --> 00:29:40.480
All of you, I have
so many questions for you,

00:29:40.480 --> 00:29:42.200
Lauren first.

00:29:42.200 --> 00:29:43.560
Thank you for introducing

00:29:43.560 --> 00:29:45.320
so many new Witchers
to the universe,

00:29:45.320 --> 00:29:48.560
only to brutally murder them
in Season 2.

00:29:48.560 --> 00:29:50.160
-(laughter)
-That was awesome.

00:29:50.160 --> 00:29:52.360
Why did you do that?

00:29:52.360 --> 00:29:56.200
I mean, so much of Season 2
is about the fact that, uh...

00:29:56.200 --> 00:29:58.160
is the Witcher brotherhood over?

00:29:58.160 --> 00:29:59.640
I mean, that's really what
Vesemir's character

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:01.240
is dealing with all season,

00:30:01.240 --> 00:30:02.640
is this emotional sense of,

00:30:02.640 --> 00:30:06.840
"Is this the end of life
as we know it for Witchers?"

00:30:06.840 --> 00:30:08.880
So, of course,
we had to kill more.

00:30:08.880 --> 00:30:11.560
We had to make it
more painful for him.

00:30:11.560 --> 00:30:13.120
-Thank you.
-Sorry.

00:30:13.120 --> 00:30:14.800
(laughter)

00:30:14.800 --> 00:30:16.280
We just wanted to make sure
that we were really

00:30:16.280 --> 00:30:17.560
driving home that point,

00:30:17.560 --> 00:30:19.080
that the Continent is
constantly changing,

00:30:19.080 --> 00:30:21.080
and is there a place
for Witchers anymore?

00:30:21.080 --> 00:30:22.880
You clearly have
a love for expanding

00:30:22.880 --> 00:30:24.440
on the Witcher universe,

00:30:24.440 --> 00:30:26.240
both in Season 2,

00:30:26.240 --> 00:30:27.760
in Nightmare of the Wolf anime.

00:30:27.760 --> 00:30:30.440
It's amazing, watch it if
you haven't seen it, audience.

00:30:30.440 --> 00:30:32.560
So talk us through
how you established

00:30:32.560 --> 00:30:36.080
the Witcher characters portrayed
by these lovely gentlemen here.

00:30:36.080 --> 00:30:39.280
What was important to capture
with each of their characters?

00:30:39.280 --> 00:30:40.920
So we really went
back to the books

00:30:40.920 --> 00:30:42.360
for all three
of these characters,

00:30:42.360 --> 00:30:44.160
and the most important
thing to me is that

00:30:44.160 --> 00:30:46.520
the brotherhood felt like
individuals that were

00:30:46.520 --> 00:30:48.120
part of an entity

00:30:48.120 --> 00:30:49.920
and not just an entity.

00:30:49.920 --> 00:30:51.920
So it was really
important to me,

00:30:51.920 --> 00:30:53.640
when Geralt goes back
to his childhood home,

00:30:53.640 --> 00:30:55.720
that we understand that
he has different relationships

00:30:55.720 --> 00:30:56.960
with all of these gentlemen,

00:30:56.960 --> 00:30:58.840
and they have different
relationships with him.

00:30:58.840 --> 00:31:00.000
There's jealousy there,

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:01.560
there's love there,

00:31:01.560 --> 00:31:03.440
there's disappointment
there at times,

00:31:03.440 --> 00:31:04.920
and then we wanted
to make sure

00:31:04.920 --> 00:31:07.800
that those relationships
carried over to Ciri.

00:31:07.800 --> 00:31:10.080
Um, Paul,
we see you helping Ciri

00:31:10.080 --> 00:31:12.880
with her Witcher training
a lot this season.

00:31:12.880 --> 00:31:15.400
Talk to me about
all those obstacle course scenes

00:31:15.400 --> 00:31:16.720
at Kaer Morhen.

00:31:16.720 --> 00:31:18.400
Did you enjoy
watching Freya suffer

00:31:18.400 --> 00:31:20.920
as much as Lambert loves
torturing Ciri?

00:31:20.920 --> 00:31:22.600
Because you loved it a lot.

00:31:22.600 --> 00:31:26.200
It was my favorite thing
to film, definitely.

00:31:26.200 --> 00:31:30.960
And my relationship with Freya
was full of laughter,

00:31:30.960 --> 00:31:34.120
so we were able to kind of
laugh our way through it.

00:31:34.120 --> 00:31:37.440
But I was just
very impressed by Freya

00:31:37.440 --> 00:31:39.400
in that whole sequence.

00:31:39.400 --> 00:31:41.080
I'd look up,

00:31:41.080 --> 00:31:42.880
I'd see her stunt double
lining up the shot,

00:31:42.880 --> 00:31:45.360
and then I'll be
chatting away to Yas,

00:31:45.360 --> 00:31:46.840
and I'll look back up
and I'll go,

00:31:46.840 --> 00:31:48.440
"Oh no, wait--it's Freya."

00:31:48.440 --> 00:31:51.960
Like, she was just
so brave and consistent

00:31:51.960 --> 00:31:55.840
in everything that she did
that sequence.

00:31:55.840 --> 00:31:57.600
And yeah, I was like, "No,
that's not her stunt double.

00:31:57.600 --> 00:32:00.320
That's Freya hanging
in a harness,

00:32:00.320 --> 00:32:01.480
throwing herself around."

00:32:01.480 --> 00:32:03.920
Like, it's just
very impressive, fearless,

00:32:03.920 --> 00:32:07.680
and yeah, my hat's off to her,
she really is brilliant.

00:32:07.680 --> 00:32:09.880
Yeah, and you know,
that quality of love

00:32:09.880 --> 00:32:12.080
underneath the gruff
sort of toughness

00:32:12.080 --> 00:32:14.480
really made your character
likable in a way

00:32:14.480 --> 00:32:16.880
where I think
it could have gone bad.

00:32:16.880 --> 00:32:18.240
People could have been, like,
"I don't like that guy.

00:32:18.240 --> 00:32:19.600
He's being mean to her."

00:32:19.600 --> 00:32:21.480
But really, those layers that
you add in your performance

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:25.960
really brought out
a sense of mentorship.

00:32:25.960 --> 00:32:27.760
Oh, I appreciate that.

00:32:27.760 --> 00:32:28.960
Thanks for your kind words.

00:32:28.960 --> 00:32:32.080
I just...I always feel
like people,

00:32:32.080 --> 00:32:33.400
they're not just bad.

00:32:33.400 --> 00:32:34.800
They behave like that
for a reason,

00:32:34.800 --> 00:32:37.200
and it normally comes
from a place of insecurity.

00:32:37.200 --> 00:32:40.840
So I spoke a lot about
why Lambert is the way he is,

00:32:40.840 --> 00:32:42.440
a bit of
an avoidant personality.

00:32:42.440 --> 00:32:43.400
He likes what he likes,

00:32:43.400 --> 00:32:45.440
he doesn't really like change.

00:32:45.440 --> 00:32:50.200
And Ciri really
challenges Lambert

00:32:50.200 --> 00:32:52.280
in how he reacts to things.

00:32:52.280 --> 00:32:53.640
She doesn't go anywhere.

00:32:53.640 --> 00:32:55.680
You know, he's used
to having people back down.

00:32:55.680 --> 00:32:57.360
He's a big, gruff Witcher.

00:32:57.360 --> 00:32:59.280
So if I say,
"You don't wanna do this"

00:32:59.280 --> 00:33:01.120
or "You shouldn't
wanna do this,"

00:33:01.120 --> 00:33:01.800
you expect someone
to back down.

00:33:01.800 --> 00:33:05.240
But, you know,
Ciri being Ciri'll say,

00:33:05.240 --> 00:33:06.880
"I'm not going anywhere.

00:33:06.880 --> 00:33:08.720
I want to be a Witcher.

00:33:08.720 --> 00:33:10.200
I want to do
what a Witcher does."

00:33:10.200 --> 00:33:11.560
And I think
he's not used to that,

00:33:11.560 --> 00:33:14.280
so it really challenges
how he reacts

00:33:14.280 --> 00:33:15.600
and how he sees things,

00:33:15.600 --> 00:33:18.200
and that's why I think Ciri is
such an important character

00:33:18.200 --> 00:33:19.640
to Lambert, I think.

00:33:19.640 --> 00:33:22.120
Yeah, she really pushes
everyone's growth a little bit.

00:33:22.120 --> 00:33:24.760
Uh, Yasen, if Lambert is
the pain-in-the-neck brother

00:33:24.760 --> 00:33:26.320
to Ciri,

00:33:26.320 --> 00:33:29.000
you are definitely the kinder,
more considerate sibling.

00:33:29.000 --> 00:33:31.680
We see Coen take Ciri
under his wing in Kaer Morhen.

00:33:31.680 --> 00:33:34.400
Do you think Coen's caring side
goes against

00:33:34.400 --> 00:33:36.800
the typical Witcher stereotype?

00:33:36.800 --> 00:33:39.440
What I find fascinating
about that question is,

00:33:39.440 --> 00:33:40.760
throughout the show,

00:33:40.760 --> 00:33:42.840
throughout Season 1
and Season 2,

00:33:42.840 --> 00:33:45.360
we hear that Witchers
don't have emotions

00:33:45.360 --> 00:33:46.680
and it goes against everything.

00:33:46.680 --> 00:33:48.800
But what we see is
something completely different.

00:33:48.800 --> 00:33:50.760
You know, we follow Geralt

00:33:50.760 --> 00:33:54.480
searching for this girl
throughout the whole season,

00:33:54.480 --> 00:33:55.720
and then we see his love.

00:33:55.720 --> 00:33:57.360
And then,
we meet Vesemir

00:33:57.360 --> 00:33:59.480
and we see the love
that he has for his children,

00:33:59.480 --> 00:34:01.120
you know?

00:34:01.120 --> 00:34:02.840
And it's always
strange to me.

00:34:02.840 --> 00:34:05.320
We hear it, but what we see
is completely different.

00:34:05.320 --> 00:34:07.480
Kim, I was super excited
to see that anime

00:34:07.480 --> 00:34:10.360
and get to know a little bit
more about Vesemir's back story.

00:34:10.360 --> 00:34:11.600
How is it playing
such a character

00:34:11.600 --> 00:34:13.960
with a rich back story
like this?

00:34:13.960 --> 00:34:15.320
Thank you for doing that,

00:34:15.320 --> 00:34:18.080
because you told me
when we were shooting

00:34:18.080 --> 00:34:20.120
that you wanted to make--

00:34:20.120 --> 00:34:21.960
this is a family story,

00:34:21.960 --> 00:34:25.200
and we're going deeper
into the emotional stuff,

00:34:25.200 --> 00:34:28.520
and we're gonna tell everybody
who Vesemir was

00:34:28.520 --> 00:34:29.960
when he was young.

00:34:29.960 --> 00:34:31.400
I was so happy to see it
when it came out.

00:34:31.400 --> 00:34:34.040
It was so fun to be young
as Vesemir.

00:34:34.040 --> 00:34:36.120
I almost forgot
how much fun I had.

00:34:36.120 --> 00:34:37.480
(laughter)

00:34:37.480 --> 00:34:41.560
And-and-and I was very,
very, very proud to see

00:34:41.560 --> 00:34:43.520
that it was so well-made.

00:34:43.520 --> 00:34:46.560
I love manga drawing
and the music,

00:34:46.560 --> 00:34:48.680
and the voice of his,
the timing,

00:34:48.680 --> 00:34:51.640
and connecting with
American and Japanese styles

00:34:51.640 --> 00:34:53.160
was incredibly good.

00:34:53.160 --> 00:34:57.280
So I was so happy
that I was allowed to see that.

00:34:57.280 --> 00:35:00.560
And did you see it
before you acted on--

00:35:00.560 --> 00:35:02.280
or did you see it
after filming?

00:35:02.280 --> 00:35:03.920
I saw it when it came out,
like you guys,

00:35:03.920 --> 00:35:08.440
so it was after I had been
doing the job for Vesemir, yes.

00:35:08.440 --> 00:35:10.360
And I think, you know,
what you said, Yas,

00:35:10.360 --> 00:35:11.840
is really, really important,

00:35:11.840 --> 00:35:14.120
which is that Witchers are known
to not have emotions,

00:35:14.120 --> 00:35:16.480
which is something that
humans have kind of concocted

00:35:16.480 --> 00:35:18.240
so that they can treat
Witchers poorly.

00:35:18.240 --> 00:35:20.280
But Witchers have taken on
that mantle,

00:35:20.280 --> 00:35:22.040
because it allows them
to do their jobs

00:35:22.040 --> 00:35:24.240
without really caring
about any effects.

00:35:24.240 --> 00:35:26.120
They can just go in
and kill and say,

00:35:26.120 --> 00:35:27.640
"I do it just for money,
no other reason,

00:35:27.640 --> 00:35:29.440
and I can leave."

00:35:29.440 --> 00:35:32.640
And what I think we explore
with Vesemir's character

00:35:32.640 --> 00:35:34.600
is just how not true that is.

00:35:34.600 --> 00:35:37.880
And there is this history
in Vesemir.

00:35:37.880 --> 00:35:39.840
He is the keeper
of Witcher history.

00:35:39.840 --> 00:35:41.880
It's something that's explored
in Nightmare of the Wolf

00:35:41.880 --> 00:35:43.560
a lot more,

00:35:43.560 --> 00:35:45.080
but we wanted
to continue it here,

00:35:45.080 --> 00:35:47.040
and I think
one of my favorite scenes is--

00:35:47.040 --> 00:35:48.560
and you'll know
the one I'm talking about--

00:35:48.560 --> 00:35:50.360
it's you and Freya,

00:35:50.360 --> 00:35:54.760
it is Ciri and Vesemir walking
through the Kaer Morhen lab,

00:35:54.760 --> 00:35:59.360
and we hear Vesemir's retelling
of the deaths of the Witchers,

00:35:59.360 --> 00:36:03.800
and why suddenly he thinks
that Ciri could be their savior.

00:36:03.800 --> 00:36:05.880
And it's that type of optimism

00:36:05.880 --> 00:36:08.400
that is really important
to Vesemir.

00:36:08.400 --> 00:36:10.960
Eskel's death in Episode 2.

00:36:10.960 --> 00:36:12.880
-Yes.
-It was very traumatic.

00:36:12.880 --> 00:36:16.320
Can you explain your decision
to kill him off so soon?

00:36:16.320 --> 00:36:18.080
Because people want to know.

00:36:18.080 --> 00:36:19.480
Yes, people definitely
want to know.

00:36:19.480 --> 00:36:21.000
Um, a couple of things.

00:36:21.000 --> 00:36:23.160
Well, we--we knew we had to kill
someone in that episode.

00:36:23.160 --> 00:36:25.480
We knew that we wanted
a monster to enter Kaer Morhen

00:36:25.480 --> 00:36:27.200
and have something to do
with Ciri,

00:36:27.200 --> 00:36:30.560
and both Geralt and Vesemir
and the brothers realize

00:36:30.560 --> 00:36:33.760
that bringing this girl
into their Witcher keep

00:36:33.760 --> 00:36:35.800
is going to fundamentally
change things.

00:36:35.800 --> 00:36:37.160
And in all honesty,

00:36:37.160 --> 00:36:39.080
the very first version
of the script we wrote

00:36:39.080 --> 00:36:41.280
was a brand-new Witcher
that we'd never met before,

00:36:41.280 --> 00:36:42.600
we'd never heard of,

00:36:42.600 --> 00:36:44.160
and all of a sudden,
we were like,

00:36:44.160 --> 00:36:45.920
"Oh. Our audience
is going to meet

00:36:45.920 --> 00:36:49.440
Coen and Lambert and Eskel
and John.

00:36:49.440 --> 00:36:50.440
Who's going to die?

00:36:50.440 --> 00:36:52.200
John is going to die."

00:36:52.200 --> 00:36:55.000
So we thought about it
really hard,

00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:57.720
and I know that there are
fans who love Eskel

00:36:57.720 --> 00:37:00.480
and who feel like,
why would we do that?

00:37:00.480 --> 00:37:03.680
But honestly, his death is what
changes everything for Geralt,

00:37:03.680 --> 00:37:06.600
and I think it propels
Geralt's need to figure out

00:37:06.600 --> 00:37:09.080
what's going on with Ciri
and to do it fast,

00:37:09.080 --> 00:37:11.120
because he knows that he has--

00:37:11.120 --> 00:37:13.760
he's gonna risk losing her
and his brothers if he doesn't.

00:37:13.760 --> 00:37:15.400
And we really just
wanted to motivate

00:37:15.400 --> 00:37:16.960
that character journey for him.

00:37:16.960 --> 00:37:18.320
Yeah. Well,
you don't kill somebody

00:37:18.320 --> 00:37:19.920
unless people care about them,
right?

00:37:19.920 --> 00:37:21.760
-It's true.
-It's an honor, right?

00:37:21.760 --> 00:37:23.840
-That's what they tell you.
-Yes.

00:37:23.840 --> 00:37:25.760
Because as an actor,
you love to hear that.

00:37:25.760 --> 00:37:27.280
Also, it keeps us on our toes,

00:37:27.280 --> 00:37:29.480
because now, we're like,
"No one's safe."

00:37:29.480 --> 00:37:31.240
Absolutely.

00:37:31.240 --> 00:37:32.640
You're scrolling
through the PDF,

00:37:32.640 --> 00:37:34.040
"Am I dead? Am I dead?"

00:37:34.040 --> 00:37:35.480
Every time I got
a script through,

00:37:35.480 --> 00:37:36.880
I was going,
"Ohhh no..."

00:37:36.880 --> 00:37:38.720
(laughter)

00:37:38.720 --> 00:37:40.040
-It was like, "Noo..."
-And I'd get a phone call

00:37:40.040 --> 00:37:42.040
as well from him.

00:37:42.040 --> 00:37:43.720
"Hello,
have you read anything?"

00:37:43.720 --> 00:37:45.200
Well, you guys survived,

00:37:45.200 --> 00:37:46.720
and the audience cannot wait
to see more of you.

00:37:46.720 --> 00:37:48.800
So a lot of fun
went down at Kaer Morhen,

00:37:48.800 --> 00:37:50.320
but it built up
to what we could call

00:37:50.320 --> 00:37:52.160
the monster match at the end,

00:37:52.160 --> 00:37:55.480
Witchers vs basilisks
vs Voleth Ciri.

00:37:55.480 --> 00:37:58.960
Talk us through shooting
that epic fight together.

00:37:58.960 --> 00:38:00.800
How were the basilisks on set?

00:38:00.800 --> 00:38:02.560
Were they just super divas?

00:38:02.560 --> 00:38:03.800
Oh, I loved it.

00:38:03.800 --> 00:38:06.200
I loved it because you're
doing something so epic,

00:38:06.200 --> 00:38:08.600
and you know
that it's gonna look epic.

00:38:08.600 --> 00:38:13.560
But essentially, you've got,
like, stuntmen in spandex

00:38:13.560 --> 00:38:18.160
operating puppet heads,
foam puppet heads,

00:38:18.160 --> 00:38:19.720
all in bright green.

00:38:19.720 --> 00:38:22.160
So I sometimes would giggle,

00:38:22.160 --> 00:38:24.520
because I would catch myself
trying to be

00:38:24.520 --> 00:38:26.800
all stoic and serious,
and then,

00:38:26.800 --> 00:38:31.080
there's a guy in green spandex
in front of me.

00:38:31.080 --> 00:38:32.800
But then you know, like,

00:38:32.800 --> 00:38:35.720
when I saw it in ADR
and I was like,

00:38:35.720 --> 00:38:38.320
"Woo! Okay, great."

00:38:38.320 --> 00:38:39.400
But it was brilliant.

00:38:39.400 --> 00:38:41.160
And, you know, long days,

00:38:41.160 --> 00:38:42.480
but we were able to--

00:38:42.480 --> 00:38:44.560
I think everybody was just
so hyped to be doing it.

00:38:44.560 --> 00:38:45.960
It was lovely.

00:38:45.960 --> 00:38:48.320
Yasen, had you ever done
that kind of fighting before?

00:38:48.320 --> 00:38:49.600
-Not like that.
-Okay.

00:38:49.600 --> 00:38:50.960
Not like that.

00:38:50.960 --> 00:38:54.040
I remember I had a small kip

00:38:54.040 --> 00:38:57.480
before we went to do
one of them.

00:38:57.480 --> 00:38:59.640
The first time, actually,
that they were on set, right?

00:38:59.640 --> 00:39:02.800
-Yeah.
-And I was...I woke up,

00:39:02.800 --> 00:39:04.000
and I was still in a daze,

00:39:04.000 --> 00:39:05.520
and I was, like,
checks and everything.

00:39:05.520 --> 00:39:07.000
I walked onto set,
and like you said,

00:39:07.000 --> 00:39:10.040
I just saw two, like,
green condoms just...

00:39:10.040 --> 00:39:12.000
(laughter)

00:39:12.000 --> 00:39:13.240
...just staring me in the face,

00:39:13.240 --> 00:39:14.840
and I was still, like,
trying to just kinda

00:39:14.840 --> 00:39:16.080
wake up a little bit.

00:39:16.080 --> 00:39:17.680
I thought, "What on earth
is going on here

00:39:17.680 --> 00:39:19.040
with these big heads?"

00:39:19.040 --> 00:39:20.400
And they were like,
"We're gonna fight that."

00:39:20.400 --> 00:39:22.160
And I was like, "All right."

00:39:22.160 --> 00:39:23.680
And then I said to Lambert,
"What the hell is that?"

00:39:23.680 --> 00:39:25.400
He was like...

00:39:25.400 --> 00:39:28.760
I just went,
"They're basilisks, Coen."

00:39:28.760 --> 00:39:30.600
I was like, "Okay."

00:39:30.600 --> 00:39:32.640
Kim, you had so many
wonderful dramatic moments

00:39:32.640 --> 00:39:33.920
during those fight sequences.

00:39:33.920 --> 00:39:37.120
How do you ground yourself
in the reality of your character

00:39:37.120 --> 00:39:39.720
when all these--I mean,
they are kinda silly things

00:39:39.720 --> 00:39:41.240
are happening on set,

00:39:41.240 --> 00:39:42.560
but you really have
to find your reality,

00:39:42.560 --> 00:39:44.600
and these kind of
ridiculous things

00:39:44.600 --> 00:39:45.840
that the audience
will never see,

00:39:45.840 --> 00:39:47.760
but that's what you're
experiencing as a person.

00:39:47.760 --> 00:39:49.280
How do you ground yourself?

00:39:49.280 --> 00:39:51.320
I love the illusion world.

00:39:51.320 --> 00:39:54.240
I love to visualize everything,

00:39:54.240 --> 00:39:57.480
and they're so good today
that you can see everything

00:39:57.480 --> 00:40:00.040
on iPads, what's going on.

00:40:00.040 --> 00:40:02.920
But, I mean,
you are in a room that,

00:40:02.920 --> 00:40:05.240
as Lauren said,
is built one to one,

00:40:05.240 --> 00:40:09.000
so you really get the illusion
how big these monsters are.

00:40:09.000 --> 00:40:12.120
Also knowing,
Lauren told me,

00:40:12.120 --> 00:40:14.000
"We need your emotional stuff.
Come on,"

00:40:14.000 --> 00:40:16.640
and you just gave me
more and more to work with,

00:40:16.640 --> 00:40:18.200
it was, like, scary!

00:40:18.200 --> 00:40:21.000
The last fight is scary,
it's so scary for me,

00:40:21.000 --> 00:40:23.440
it's like, why?

00:40:23.440 --> 00:40:26.040
Why didn't I see that come?

00:40:26.040 --> 00:40:28.160
Well, it really grounds
the whole sequence.

00:40:28.160 --> 00:40:31.200
I mean, it's not only
spectacular visual-wise

00:40:31.200 --> 00:40:32.640
but also emotionally.

00:40:32.640 --> 00:40:35.240
There's so many wonderful
culminations of the season.

00:40:35.240 --> 00:40:36.560
And we really wanted that.

00:40:36.560 --> 00:40:38.320
We really wanted
to put that in

00:40:38.320 --> 00:40:41.160
as a statement,

00:40:41.160 --> 00:40:43.280
because it's not normal
you see so much feeling

00:40:43.280 --> 00:40:44.800
in such a big fight,
you know,

00:40:44.800 --> 00:40:46.160
because it has to go fast.

00:40:46.160 --> 00:40:49.040
I remember when you put it
together, and saying,

00:40:49.040 --> 00:40:51.920
"These characters have to show
the emotional stuff into it,"

00:40:51.920 --> 00:40:53.280
so it's like a break.

00:40:53.280 --> 00:40:59.040
It's like you stop time
to go into our emotional stuff,

00:40:59.040 --> 00:41:01.400
and that was incredible,
as an actor,

00:41:01.400 --> 00:41:03.720
to have the opportunity to--

00:41:03.720 --> 00:41:06.840
then you couldn't let it go,
you had to follow it.

00:41:06.840 --> 00:41:09.440
It's like you're fighting
and it's going so fast,

00:41:09.440 --> 00:41:10.840
and you really don't know
what's going on,

00:41:10.840 --> 00:41:12.880
and then it stops,
time stops!

00:41:12.880 --> 00:41:14.200
(mimics explosion sound)

00:41:14.200 --> 00:41:15.760
And you have all your time.

00:41:15.760 --> 00:41:18.040
-It's so wonderful.
-It's so crazy!

00:41:18.040 --> 00:41:20.800
Was there a specific challenge,
Lauren,

00:41:20.800 --> 00:41:24.400
in crafting that finale that
you wanna show the audience?

00:41:24.400 --> 00:41:26.200
I mean, I can't imagine
it was easy in any way,

00:41:26.200 --> 00:41:28.480
but what was
the biggest challenge?

00:41:28.480 --> 00:41:30.960
Um, there were so,
so many challenges.

00:41:30.960 --> 00:41:33.200
I mean, COVID,
let's start with that.

00:41:33.200 --> 00:41:36.240
But no, I mean, I think
Kim kind of landed on it,

00:41:36.240 --> 00:41:38.360
which is,
I think that so often,

00:41:38.360 --> 00:41:40.560
epic battles lose
their emotionality,

00:41:40.560 --> 00:41:42.600
and yet, what we learned,
honestly,

00:41:42.600 --> 00:41:44.720
through our fight sequences
in Season 1,

00:41:44.720 --> 00:41:47.280
is that without changing--

00:41:47.280 --> 00:41:48.920
if a battle doesn't
change a character,

00:41:48.920 --> 00:41:50.400
if a fight doesn't
change a character,

00:41:50.400 --> 00:41:51.760
then it's not worth doing.

00:41:51.760 --> 00:41:53.360
And Kim is absolutely right.

00:41:53.360 --> 00:41:55.720
So much of a battle
is about frenetic pace,

00:41:55.720 --> 00:41:58.120
is about making sure
that it looks cool,

00:41:58.120 --> 00:41:59.400
and yet, constantly,

00:41:59.400 --> 00:42:01.280
we'd be stopping on set
to say, "No.

00:42:01.280 --> 00:42:03.720
We need to land on Vesemir
in this moment.

00:42:03.720 --> 00:42:05.760
We need to land
on Lambert and Coen

00:42:05.760 --> 00:42:07.600
as they're realizing
that these monsters

00:42:07.600 --> 00:42:09.400
are coming into their home,

00:42:09.400 --> 00:42:11.080
and that shouldn't be able
to happen."

00:42:11.080 --> 00:42:13.920
So it really was
about timing it all out

00:42:13.920 --> 00:42:16.080
and asking
these brilliant actors

00:42:16.080 --> 00:42:19.400
to trust what felt like
a weird rhythm on the day,

00:42:19.400 --> 00:42:20.880
that we would be able
to build it,

00:42:20.880 --> 00:42:22.920
and I think, I mean, honestly,
I think it's the biggest thing

00:42:22.920 --> 00:42:24.840
we've ever done
and one of my favorites.

00:42:24.840 --> 00:42:26.040
It's incredibly epic,

00:42:26.040 --> 00:42:28.960
and because those
emotional things were in it,

00:42:28.960 --> 00:42:30.720
you remember it more.

00:42:30.720 --> 00:42:33.760
Kim, Vesemir's most pressing
concern throughout the season

00:42:33.760 --> 00:42:36.000
is continuing
the Witcher way of life.

00:42:36.000 --> 00:42:37.160
In the final showdown,

00:42:37.160 --> 00:42:39.040
we sadly saw a lot
of Witcher deaths,

00:42:39.040 --> 00:42:41.520
so do you think Vesemir
has given up on his plan

00:42:41.520 --> 00:42:46.160
to use Ciri's Elder Blood
to try to make more Witchers?

00:42:46.160 --> 00:42:47.160
No.

00:42:47.160 --> 00:42:48.200
(laughter)

00:42:48.200 --> 00:42:49.560
No, definitely not.

00:42:49.560 --> 00:42:52.040
I'm thinking about it
all the time,

00:42:52.040 --> 00:42:53.360
even now.

00:42:53.360 --> 00:42:55.120
It's so grounded in me

00:42:55.120 --> 00:42:57.720
that we have to continue
going on,

00:42:57.720 --> 00:43:01.320
especially now when monsters
are really taking over.

00:43:01.320 --> 00:43:04.640
There is a reason why we are
here in the Witcher world,

00:43:04.640 --> 00:43:09.400
you know, and it's not getting
less necessary to have them now.

00:43:09.400 --> 00:43:13.800
So if we really need
a light in the darkness

00:43:13.800 --> 00:43:17.600
and a hope,
we should get back now.

00:43:17.600 --> 00:43:18.800
We can't wait.

00:43:18.800 --> 00:43:21.360
It's like,
and this Fire-fucker,

00:43:21.360 --> 00:43:22.960
what the fuck, you know?

00:43:22.960 --> 00:43:25.720
He's still--he's a pain
in the ass for me, you know?

00:43:25.720 --> 00:43:27.960
I was like, you know,
that's why I'm crying

00:43:27.960 --> 00:43:30.400
in front of the fireworks,
what the fuck?

00:43:30.400 --> 00:43:32.040
-I mean...
-Yeah.

00:43:32.040 --> 00:43:35.440
Well, I can't wait to see
those story points come to life,

00:43:35.440 --> 00:43:38.360
especially you
and the Fire-fucker.

00:43:38.360 --> 00:43:40.560
At last, what would you two
like to see

00:43:40.560 --> 00:43:42.040
from Lambert and Coen
in the future?

00:43:42.040 --> 00:43:44.080
You have your opportunity,
the showrunner right now,

00:43:44.080 --> 00:43:47.200
pitch your Season 3 ideas.

00:43:47.200 --> 00:43:49.040
Okay, Lauren, open mind.

00:43:49.040 --> 00:43:51.080
-Okay.
-Okay, I want you to think

00:43:51.080 --> 00:43:53.360
fantasy meets sci-fi,

00:43:53.360 --> 00:43:55.560
so basically,

00:43:55.560 --> 00:43:58.080
Witchers and space.

00:43:58.080 --> 00:43:59.840
We've actually got something
for you.

00:43:59.840 --> 00:44:01.600
-Oh God.
-Like, literally we have

00:44:01.600 --> 00:44:03.000
a script/treatment.

00:44:03.000 --> 00:44:05.880
-Okay.
-And it's called

00:44:05.880 --> 00:44:08.120
Coen and Lambchop,
Episode 1.

00:44:08.120 --> 00:44:10.160
No, no, this is ser--
like, this is not--

00:44:10.160 --> 00:44:12.520
this is not funny,
otherwise we'll go to Disney.

00:44:12.520 --> 00:44:14.480
-Okay, okay.
-As simple as that.

00:44:14.480 --> 00:44:16.800
-We're meeting Disney next week.
-Please, keep an open mind.

00:44:16.800 --> 00:44:19.160
-Okay.
-Read it. Vesemir survives.

00:44:19.160 --> 00:44:22.360
I'm not sure about Geralt
and Yennefer and everybody else.

00:44:22.360 --> 00:44:23.800
Jaskier is in it.

00:44:23.800 --> 00:44:25.800
He turns into a lizard, but
we'll come back to that later.

00:44:25.800 --> 00:44:28.840
I see words, "lizard,
rare cell disease."

00:44:28.840 --> 00:44:30.800
-"Twin sister"?!
-Yes!

00:44:30.800 --> 00:44:32.640
On the planet Bantil, yes.

00:44:32.640 --> 00:44:34.320
Give me that,
I'll hide it for you

00:44:34.320 --> 00:44:35.920
to awkwardly throw away later.

00:44:35.920 --> 00:44:37.000
(laughter)

00:44:37.000 --> 00:44:38.520
-We tried.
-We tried.

00:44:38.520 --> 00:44:39.880
You tried, and I admire you.

00:44:39.880 --> 00:44:41.960
Every actor in the world
admires you right now.

00:44:41.960 --> 00:44:43.000
(laughter)

00:44:43.000 --> 00:44:44.800
Thank you so much,
it's been such an honor.

00:44:44.800 --> 00:44:46.800
Lauren, I'm just gonna keep you
a little bit longer.

00:44:46.800 --> 00:44:48.600
-Thank you, Witchers.
-Thank you.

00:44:48.600 --> 00:44:57.520
♪

00:44:57.520 --> 00:44:58.840
Kill them both!

00:44:58.840 --> 00:45:00.600
(shouting, swords clanging)

00:45:00.600 --> 00:45:01.760
♪

00:45:01.760 --> 00:45:03.520
Time for another deleted scene!

00:45:03.520 --> 00:45:05.760
You can't say
we aren't spoiling you.

00:45:05.760 --> 00:45:08.120
Let's check in with the mages
of Season 2

00:45:08.120 --> 00:45:09.920
and a clip from Aretuza.

00:45:09.920 --> 00:45:12.480
(fire crackling)

00:45:16.000 --> 00:45:17.880
(door closes)

00:45:17.880 --> 00:45:22.040
You came late to
our fold, Vilgefortz.

00:45:22.040 --> 00:45:25.040
Born of a prostitute,
abandoned in a sewer,

00:45:25.040 --> 00:45:27.200
your magic shaped
by the ignorant druids

00:45:27.200 --> 00:45:28.920
who picked you up.

00:45:28.920 --> 00:45:30.600
Impressive, really,

00:45:30.600 --> 00:45:34.680
how high you've managed to rise
above your station.

00:45:34.680 --> 00:45:37.400
Perhaps that depth
of life experience

00:45:37.400 --> 00:45:39.880
honed my instincts,

00:45:39.880 --> 00:45:42.800
gave me the hunch
that saved your ass.

00:45:42.800 --> 00:45:45.880
And so even-tempered.

00:45:45.880 --> 00:45:48.160
You really think
you're qualified

00:45:48.160 --> 00:45:50.800
to lead the brotherhood?

00:45:50.800 --> 00:45:54.360
A bastard
who never attended Ban Ard

00:45:54.360 --> 00:45:56.720
and clearly doesn't respect
our code of ethics.

00:45:56.720 --> 00:45:59.920
Well, all mages are equal
before the law.

00:45:59.920 --> 00:46:02.560
Oh, now, now.

00:46:02.560 --> 00:46:06.200
Some are more equal than others.

00:46:06.200 --> 00:46:09.440
I'm referring to the banned use
of fire magic, of course.

00:46:09.440 --> 00:46:10.680
I was there.

00:46:10.680 --> 00:46:12.040
I know what she did.

00:46:12.040 --> 00:46:15.920
Then you also know
there's always a cost.

00:46:15.920 --> 00:46:17.880
The flames
should have destroyed her,

00:46:17.880 --> 00:46:19.600
yet she was untouched.

00:46:19.600 --> 00:46:21.920
There's something
unnatural in her.

00:46:21.920 --> 00:46:24.560
And there's something unnatural

00:46:24.560 --> 00:46:28.200
in how entrenched
your protégé is with Nilfgaard.

00:46:28.200 --> 00:46:29.880
Istredd is loyal.

00:46:29.880 --> 00:46:34.480
He was the first to inform us
of Yennefer's lineage.

00:46:34.480 --> 00:46:38.200
Even you can't be stupid enough
to believe your own rhetoric,

00:46:38.200 --> 00:46:40.360
that her Elven Blood
is a threat to us.

00:46:40.360 --> 00:46:44.120
Then what about her rage,
her tempestuousness?

00:46:44.120 --> 00:46:46.680
♪

00:46:46.680 --> 00:46:49.480
Then again,

00:46:49.480 --> 00:46:53.080
you understand those
all too well, don't you?

00:46:53.080 --> 00:47:03.040
♪

00:47:05.400 --> 00:47:07.680
Ooh, all attention!

00:47:07.680 --> 00:47:09.080
(laughter)

00:47:09.080 --> 00:47:11.720
So, Lauren,
you know all the secrets.

00:47:11.720 --> 00:47:14.200
I'm gonna ask you some questions
you probably can't answer,

00:47:14.200 --> 00:47:15.640
and I'll just have
to deal with it

00:47:15.640 --> 00:47:17.600
by crying myself to sleep
tonight.

00:47:17.600 --> 00:47:19.640
Can you please, please, please
tease anything

00:47:19.640 --> 00:47:22.200
about the moves Vilgefortz
is gonna make in Season 3?

00:47:22.200 --> 00:47:23.400
No!

00:47:23.400 --> 00:47:25.440
-I said please!
-You did say please,

00:47:25.440 --> 00:47:28.160
but what was so interesting
about this scene in particular

00:47:28.160 --> 00:47:30.640
is that we have talked a lot,
as a writers' room,

00:47:30.640 --> 00:47:33.360
about how to roll out the
information about Vilgefortz.

00:47:33.360 --> 00:47:34.920
We did know that,
in Season 1,

00:47:34.920 --> 00:47:36.680
he kind of appeared
out of nowhere,

00:47:36.680 --> 00:47:38.640
and that's because he has
a sort of deep history

00:47:38.640 --> 00:47:39.920
that we don't know about.

00:47:39.920 --> 00:47:42.400
It's just, we started to lean
into it this season

00:47:42.400 --> 00:47:45.480
and then ultimately we decided
it's way too soon.

00:47:45.480 --> 00:47:47.680
So you'll just have to wait,
you'll have to watch Season 3.

00:47:47.680 --> 00:47:49.840
I have so many questions
about the finale,

00:47:49.840 --> 00:47:52.000
and can we start by talking
a little bit

00:47:52.000 --> 00:47:53.840
about Voleth Meir for a second?

00:47:53.840 --> 00:47:55.560
She is not in the books.

00:47:55.560 --> 00:47:57.040
It was quite a finale.

00:47:57.040 --> 00:47:59.240
So where did this creature
come from?

00:47:59.240 --> 00:48:01.360
So, I can answer that
in two parts, which is,

00:48:01.360 --> 00:48:03.240
Voleth Meir herself,
the "Deathless Mother,"

00:48:03.240 --> 00:48:06.040
she's based on the mythology
of Baba Yaga,

00:48:06.040 --> 00:48:09.720
and one of the reasons that
we chose specifically Baba Yaga

00:48:09.720 --> 00:48:11.560
is we found that it was
a monster

00:48:11.560 --> 00:48:14.760
that basically every culture
has a version of.

00:48:14.760 --> 00:48:17.160
One of the things that we love
that Sapkowski does

00:48:17.160 --> 00:48:19.840
when he finds his monsters,
when he creates his stories,

00:48:19.840 --> 00:48:21.920
is he pulls from lore
all over the world.

00:48:21.920 --> 00:48:23.320
He was a traveling salesman,

00:48:23.320 --> 00:48:25.400
so when he started writing
these stories,

00:48:25.400 --> 00:48:27.840
he had all of these different
inspirations to pull from,

00:48:27.840 --> 00:48:29.280
so we always think about that

00:48:29.280 --> 00:48:30.840
when we're creating
new monsters.

00:48:30.840 --> 00:48:34.760
Now, why did we create
a new monster?

00:48:34.760 --> 00:48:37.040
So, Blood of Elves,
I have to say,

00:48:37.040 --> 00:48:40.680
was terrifying to me
in terms of adaptation.

00:48:40.680 --> 00:48:42.920
I found it so much more
difficult in some ways

00:48:42.920 --> 00:48:44.520
than Season 1
and certainly more difficult

00:48:44.520 --> 00:48:47.080
than what I know is coming
down the pike

00:48:47.080 --> 00:48:50.760
because it's a book that's a lot
about character relationships

00:48:50.760 --> 00:48:51.920
and character movements

00:48:51.920 --> 00:48:55.960
but not a lot
of forward-propelling action,

00:48:55.960 --> 00:48:58.360
and I think our fans
of The Witcher expect

00:48:58.360 --> 00:49:01.200
sort of an active rollercoaster
for eight episodes.

00:49:01.200 --> 00:49:03.680
So we started to talk
about how to introduce

00:49:03.680 --> 00:49:06.400
a season-long villain,
not just the monsters

00:49:06.400 --> 00:49:08.200
that Geralt is fighting
in every episode,

00:49:08.200 --> 00:49:10.120
and furthermore we talked
about how that villain

00:49:10.120 --> 00:49:13.560
could start to tie together
Yennefer's stories,

00:49:13.560 --> 00:49:15.240
Geralt's story, Ciri's story,

00:49:15.240 --> 00:49:17.320
without them all being,
you know,

00:49:17.320 --> 00:49:18.800
on screen together yet.

00:49:18.800 --> 00:49:20.160
So, obviously, Voleth Meir

00:49:20.160 --> 00:49:22.200
comes in and out
of all of their worlds

00:49:22.200 --> 00:49:24.000
and then collides in the finale.

00:49:24.000 --> 00:49:25.680
That's interesting you say that
about, you know,

00:49:25.680 --> 00:49:27.480
the forward momentum of the book
not being there,

00:49:27.480 --> 00:49:30.920
but really it's about weaving
a tapestry of characters

00:49:30.920 --> 00:49:33.920
who are intertwined
in such amazing ways.

00:49:33.920 --> 00:49:36.040
The intricacies
of where the storylines

00:49:36.040 --> 00:49:38.040
are going in and out,
it's just so wonderful,

00:49:38.040 --> 00:49:40.360
and by the end, you have
this incredible feeling

00:49:40.360 --> 00:49:44.360
of wholeness, of "I cannot wait
to see where all of them go."

00:49:44.360 --> 00:49:47.120
Now, let's talk a little bit
more about the other big bad,

00:49:47.120 --> 00:49:48.520
the Wild Hunt.

00:49:48.520 --> 00:49:51.200
Now, this is gonna be such
a huge talking point for fans,

00:49:51.200 --> 00:49:52.560
as you probably know.

00:49:52.560 --> 00:49:54.760
Talk to me about bringing them
into this season

00:49:54.760 --> 00:49:56.560
and what you're teasing
with the promise

00:49:56.560 --> 00:49:58.600
that Ciri "belongs" to them.

00:49:58.600 --> 00:50:00.280
Right, so, the Wild Hunt

00:50:00.280 --> 00:50:02.000
is notoriously
not really introduced

00:50:02.000 --> 00:50:05.000
in the books
until the Time of Contempt,

00:50:05.000 --> 00:50:07.120
which is honestly
my favorite book in the saga

00:50:07.120 --> 00:50:09.680
and I cannot, cannot wait
to adapt it,

00:50:09.680 --> 00:50:11.840
but because the Wild Hunt
is so familiar

00:50:11.840 --> 00:50:13.080
to so many of our fans--

00:50:13.080 --> 00:50:16.440
video game fans, honestly,
The Witcher 3--

00:50:16.440 --> 00:50:19.520
we started to plant the lore
of the Wild Hunt

00:50:19.520 --> 00:50:21.440
as early as the pilot.

00:50:21.440 --> 00:50:24.680
You know, Eist actually refers
to the Wraiths of Mörhogg,

00:50:24.680 --> 00:50:28.320
and we get the sense
that they are this omen of war.

00:50:28.320 --> 00:50:31.360
You know, in Season 2,
Nivellen, you know,

00:50:31.360 --> 00:50:34.040
talks about like, basically,
they precede doom.

00:50:34.040 --> 00:50:36.280
"A portent of doom," he says.

00:50:36.280 --> 00:50:39.280
And so we wanted to make sure
that by the time we met them,

00:50:39.280 --> 00:50:41.720
honestly, we were
thoroughly scared of them.

00:50:41.720 --> 00:50:45.400
So we started playing
with the idea of what are they?

00:50:45.400 --> 00:50:47.640
We know from the books
that they are spectral,

00:50:47.640 --> 00:50:49.440
but we also know
that by the time

00:50:49.440 --> 00:50:53.080
they actually kind of interact
with Ciri more,

00:50:53.080 --> 00:50:55.120
there's a bit more tangibility
to them.

00:50:55.120 --> 00:50:57.480
So that's what we wanted
to start playing this season,

00:50:57.480 --> 00:51:00.880
which is this idea
of these ghosts in the sky.

00:51:00.880 --> 00:51:02.920
Some people believe in them,
some people don't.

00:51:02.920 --> 00:51:04.800
They're more just this,
you know,

00:51:04.800 --> 00:51:06.280
urban legend, if you will.

00:51:06.280 --> 00:51:09.760
And when they tease
that Ciri belongs to them,

00:51:09.760 --> 00:51:12.320
what we're hopefully gonna lead
viewers to believe

00:51:12.320 --> 00:51:14.280
is that there's
a lot more history

00:51:14.280 --> 00:51:15.840
of Ciri herself.

00:51:15.840 --> 00:51:17.960
Her lineage, her bloodline,

00:51:17.960 --> 00:51:21.200
things that we know now that
Calanthe has hidden from her,

00:51:21.200 --> 00:51:23.120
perhaps some connections
to Lara Dorren

00:51:23.120 --> 00:51:24.960
that we have teased
this season as well.

00:51:24.960 --> 00:51:28.440
So all of that is gonna come out
in the wash next season.

00:51:28.440 --> 00:51:30.600
Well, I love the fact
that you say you planted--

00:51:30.600 --> 00:51:33.400
I saw that, I mean,
honestly, this show,

00:51:33.400 --> 00:51:35.720
the more you watch it,
the more I admire

00:51:35.720 --> 00:51:38.080
personally and as a fan--
I'm sure fans do too--

00:51:38.080 --> 00:51:40.640
how you have planted these seeds
that come to bloom later,

00:51:40.640 --> 00:51:43.360
so much later,
and they're even just tossed off

00:51:43.360 --> 00:51:46.000
and yet they come--they are
a whole storyline later.

00:51:46.000 --> 00:51:48.760
It's so wonderful for fans.

00:51:48.760 --> 00:51:51.080
It is, most of the time,
on purpose.

00:51:51.080 --> 00:51:53.200
Sometimes just
the most amazing coincidence

00:51:53.200 --> 00:51:55.880
that we're like, "Oh, thank God
someone said that in Season 1.

00:51:55.880 --> 00:51:57.880
We're gonna make that work."

00:51:57.880 --> 00:51:59.720
Let's take a look at some
of the other characters

00:51:59.720 --> 00:52:01.240
from this season.
In our finale, we have

00:52:01.240 --> 00:52:03.760
the Head of Redanian
Intelligence, Dijkstra,

00:52:03.760 --> 00:52:06.160
scheming with Philippa Eilhart.

00:52:06.160 --> 00:52:09.120
So, these characters bring
this really awesome dynamic

00:52:09.120 --> 00:52:11.120
to the screen,
especially 'cause they seem

00:52:11.120 --> 00:52:14.200
to know everything about Ciri--
stalkering much.

00:52:14.200 --> 00:52:15.240
(laughter)

00:52:15.240 --> 00:52:16.680
Can you hint
at what you're setting up

00:52:16.680 --> 00:52:17.760
with these two characters?

00:52:17.760 --> 00:52:19.480
Absolutely, so, you know,
in Season 1,

00:52:19.480 --> 00:52:22.200
we really started to set
the political chessboard.

00:52:22.200 --> 00:52:23.800
So, Cintra was obviously
very important

00:52:23.800 --> 00:52:26.040
and we knew that Nilfgaard
was going to be important,

00:52:26.040 --> 00:52:27.760
and then we've heard
of these other kingdoms.

00:52:27.760 --> 00:52:30.840
We see Temeria a little bit
when Geralt fights the striga,

00:52:30.840 --> 00:52:34.880
but we haven't really delved
into one of the kingdoms

00:52:34.880 --> 00:52:37.680
in the way that we are going to
with Redania.

00:52:37.680 --> 00:52:40.520
And so, I wanted, again,
to start teasing

00:52:40.520 --> 00:52:41.800
who these characters were.

00:52:41.800 --> 00:52:43.920
We don't know a lot
about them this season

00:52:43.920 --> 00:52:46.080
and certainly we don't know
a lot about them personally.

00:52:46.080 --> 00:52:48.400
They're more just
sort of adversarial

00:52:48.400 --> 00:52:52.920
for Tissaia specifically,
and when Dijkstra appears

00:52:52.920 --> 00:52:55.880
at Aretuza this season,
there is the sense

00:52:55.880 --> 00:52:58.120
that he is there
kind of reading the room,

00:52:58.120 --> 00:52:59.760
and Tissaia is smart enough
to know it.

00:52:59.760 --> 00:53:01.840
He's just come with gossip,
but she knows

00:53:01.840 --> 00:53:04.720
obviously there's something
that he wants from us,

00:53:04.720 --> 00:53:06.960
and that thing is Ciri,
of course.

00:53:06.960 --> 00:53:08.480
And what we wanted to do
was make sure

00:53:08.480 --> 00:53:10.680
that by the end of the season,
we understood

00:53:10.680 --> 00:53:14.320
just how many people
are after this poor girl.

00:53:14.320 --> 00:53:16.840
You know, she feels safe
by the end of Episode 8.

00:53:16.840 --> 00:53:17.960
She has found her family,

00:53:17.960 --> 00:53:19.520
she's with Yennefer,
she's with Geralt,

00:53:19.520 --> 00:53:21.560
and it's like everything's
gonna be okay,

00:53:21.560 --> 00:53:23.440
except it's not,
it's really not.

00:53:23.440 --> 00:53:24.480
(laughter)

00:53:24.480 --> 00:53:26.080
Yeah, whenever you have
a happy moment

00:53:26.080 --> 00:53:27.320
between characters,
you're like,

00:53:27.320 --> 00:53:29.640
"Oh, that's gonna go badly
for them next season,"

00:53:29.640 --> 00:53:31.320
and in such a delicious way,
I'm sure.

00:53:31.320 --> 00:53:33.600
Yes, because it's one
of the things

00:53:33.600 --> 00:53:35.680
that we found most important
about what Sapkowski did

00:53:35.680 --> 00:53:38.240
in the novels, right,
is that every single character

00:53:38.240 --> 00:53:39.800
has some sort of underbelly.

00:53:39.800 --> 00:53:41.880
It's not even necessarily
a dark underbelly,

00:53:41.880 --> 00:53:43.440
but it's a secret
that they're hiding,

00:53:43.440 --> 00:53:44.800
it is a personal agenda,

00:53:44.800 --> 00:53:47.120
and what I love is that
we really get to play

00:53:47.120 --> 00:53:48.400
with those shades of gray.

00:53:48.400 --> 00:53:50.040
None of our characters
are black and white.

00:53:50.040 --> 00:53:54.040
Everyone, you know, none of them
are all good or all evil.

00:53:54.040 --> 00:53:56.400
We really get to play with
different sides of characters,

00:53:56.400 --> 00:53:58.040
-which is delicious.
-It is delicious,

00:53:58.040 --> 00:54:00.160
and it busts a lot of clichés
of the genre.

00:54:00.160 --> 00:54:01.280
Yeah.

00:54:01.280 --> 00:54:03.040
Lauren, it's a great reveal
in the books

00:54:03.040 --> 00:54:04.920
that Emhyr is Ciri's dad.

00:54:04.920 --> 00:54:07.960
Great reveal. Can you talk
about how you built up to it

00:54:07.960 --> 00:54:09.720
and why you wanted to end
the season

00:54:09.720 --> 00:54:11.000
on this specific twist?

00:54:11.000 --> 00:54:13.800
So this is probably the thing
that we debated most

00:54:13.800 --> 00:54:16.520
in the writers' room, and I have
so many different versions

00:54:16.520 --> 00:54:19.800
of the finale that spoiled
very different things.

00:54:19.800 --> 00:54:21.200
-Oh.
-And the truth of the matter is,

00:54:21.200 --> 00:54:23.200
we were basically divided
as a writers' room.

00:54:23.200 --> 00:54:26.000
Are we ready to let
the audiences in on this secret,

00:54:26.000 --> 00:54:27.880
especially when our characters
don't know yet?

00:54:27.880 --> 00:54:29.080
And that's always
sort of dangerous

00:54:29.080 --> 00:54:30.680
because you could feel
like your characters

00:54:30.680 --> 00:54:32.160
are behind in the story,

00:54:32.160 --> 00:54:34.840
or what we're going to lean into
is the suspense

00:54:34.840 --> 00:54:36.560
of the audience knowing
what's coming,

00:54:36.560 --> 00:54:38.120
and how and when
are our characters

00:54:38.120 --> 00:54:39.640
going to figure it out?

00:54:39.640 --> 00:54:41.760
But let's be honest,
that shift,

00:54:41.760 --> 00:54:43.480
bringing that story up,

00:54:43.480 --> 00:54:45.640
it's logistical
more than anything.

00:54:45.640 --> 00:54:48.440
We really wanted
to start to understand

00:54:48.440 --> 00:54:49.560
the Nilfgaardian Empire,

00:54:49.560 --> 00:54:51.040
and there's no way
to understand it

00:54:51.040 --> 00:54:53.480
without digging into Emhyr
a little bit more.

00:54:53.480 --> 00:54:56.560
This also goes to, you know,
the two sides of characters,

00:54:56.560 --> 00:54:58.880
right, which is,
throughout the first season,

00:54:58.880 --> 00:55:01.040
Nilfgaard is the evil empire,

00:55:01.040 --> 00:55:03.880
and yet I don't think they think
of themselves as evil.

00:55:03.880 --> 00:55:05.680
They think they're doing
the right thing,

00:55:05.680 --> 00:55:08.240
so we wanted to start to reveal
that side as well.

00:55:08.240 --> 00:55:10.640
And as soon as we see
Emhyr again,

00:55:10.640 --> 00:55:13.720
we're going to know
that he's Duny.

00:55:13.720 --> 00:55:14.960
We talked about recasting,

00:55:14.960 --> 00:55:16.800
we talked about
aging him up so much

00:55:16.800 --> 00:55:18.200
that you didn't recognize him,

00:55:18.200 --> 00:55:20.680
but those actually felt like
we were going out of our way

00:55:20.680 --> 00:55:22.160
to hide a secret
from the audience

00:55:22.160 --> 00:55:24.280
that it felt like
the right time to do it,

00:55:24.280 --> 00:55:26.440
and now we can actually dig
into Emhyr

00:55:26.440 --> 00:55:29.680
and why exactly he wants
his daughter back so badly.

00:55:29.680 --> 00:55:31.800
Absolutely, yeah,
he's a fascinating character.

00:55:31.800 --> 00:55:33.520
In the final lines
of the season,

00:55:33.520 --> 00:55:35.240
it's revealed
that Emhyr himself

00:55:35.240 --> 00:55:38.400
ordered the killing
of Francesca's baby.

00:55:38.400 --> 00:55:40.480
Can you explore
his motives for us?

00:55:40.480 --> 00:55:43.040
What is his evil, evil deal?
'Cause it's not good.

00:55:43.040 --> 00:55:44.760
It is not good,
and that's something actually

00:55:44.760 --> 00:55:46.680
that we're delving into
a lot more in Season 3,

00:55:46.680 --> 00:55:48.640
so I don't want to give that
away too much.

00:55:48.640 --> 00:55:50.840
-Okay, okay.
-But what that really did for us

00:55:50.840 --> 00:55:53.680
is it allowed us to show
just how far

00:55:53.680 --> 00:55:55.920
Fringilla and Cahir
were willing to go.

00:55:55.920 --> 00:55:59.440
You know, Fringilla has made
a friend in Francesca this year.

00:55:59.440 --> 00:56:02.840
One of my favorite scenes
is them realizing

00:56:02.840 --> 00:56:04.840
that they like being partners.

00:56:04.840 --> 00:56:07.080
There's no like
forward story movement there.

00:56:07.080 --> 00:56:10.520
It really is just about
two women actually realizing

00:56:10.520 --> 00:56:12.800
that they could be partners
and not competitors.

00:56:12.800 --> 00:56:15.600
So that was super important
to me, and yet realizing

00:56:15.600 --> 00:56:18.200
that Fringilla's sort of quest
for power

00:56:18.200 --> 00:56:20.920
may start to outweigh her desire
to have this friendship

00:56:20.920 --> 00:56:22.600
and how is she going
to do both?

00:56:22.600 --> 00:56:25.120
So we thought that was a really
interesting undercutting

00:56:25.120 --> 00:56:27.360
of what we have seen
of her journey this season,

00:56:27.360 --> 00:56:29.920
and then she needs to redeem
herself in Season 3.

00:56:29.920 --> 00:56:32.120
Yeah, that was
a very impactful scene,

00:56:32.120 --> 00:56:33.680
It was wonderfully Bechdellian.

00:56:33.680 --> 00:56:35.040
(laughter)

00:56:35.040 --> 00:56:37.640
We last properly saw Duny
in Season 1

00:56:37.640 --> 00:56:40.000
where Geralt claims
the Law of Surprise from him.

00:56:40.000 --> 00:56:41.640
Can you help us
fill in the gaps

00:56:41.640 --> 00:56:44.880
of what Duny's been doing
between Season 1 and Season 2,

00:56:44.880 --> 00:56:46.840
other than obviously
some kind of glow-up?

00:56:46.840 --> 00:56:47.840
(laughing)

00:56:47.840 --> 00:56:49.000
Serious glow-up.

00:56:49.000 --> 00:56:51.040
Um, again, that's something else

00:56:51.040 --> 00:56:53.160
that we'll talk a lot about
in Season 3

00:56:53.160 --> 00:56:56.040
because, obviously,
he has a plan.

00:56:56.040 --> 00:56:58.600
We see in Ciri's Dol Duzsa,

00:56:58.600 --> 00:57:01.480
we hear sort of the story
that Pavetta

00:57:01.480 --> 00:57:03.040
was going to get on a boat,

00:57:03.040 --> 00:57:05.040
Duny was going
to bring baby Ciri

00:57:05.040 --> 00:57:06.200
under the cover of darkness,

00:57:06.200 --> 00:57:07.720
and they were going
to sneak away.

00:57:07.720 --> 00:57:11.400
The reason being is because
people around their family

00:57:11.400 --> 00:57:14.480
were starting to realize
that Ciri, there was something

00:57:14.480 --> 00:57:16.760
a little bit bigger and better
about her,

00:57:16.760 --> 00:57:18.560
returning to the idea
of Ithlinne's prophecy

00:57:18.560 --> 00:57:20.680
that, again, is one of
those things like the Wild Hunt

00:57:20.680 --> 00:57:22.680
that we've been teasing
from the very beginning.

00:57:22.680 --> 00:57:26.040
And for book readers,
they know that that trip

00:57:26.040 --> 00:57:28.920
on the boat didn't end
exactly as planned,

00:57:28.920 --> 00:57:31.520
and that is the night
that Ciri's parents died,

00:57:31.520 --> 00:57:32.720
expect for, clearly...

00:57:32.720 --> 00:57:36.160
-"Died."
-Pavetta died and Duny did not.

00:57:36.160 --> 00:57:40.200
And Duny then had to start
to form a plan

00:57:40.200 --> 00:57:43.440
with a...with a new partner.

00:57:43.440 --> 00:57:45.800
That's what we're gonna dig into
in Season 3.

00:57:45.800 --> 00:57:47.320
Now, I have a question for you.

00:57:47.320 --> 00:57:49.240
If you noticed,
there's one of my favorite

00:57:49.240 --> 00:57:51.240
little sort of
Easter egg moments

00:57:51.240 --> 00:57:53.840
in Episode 8, which is,

00:57:53.840 --> 00:57:58.080
Ciri is in the illusion fantasy

00:57:58.080 --> 00:58:01.360
and she is starting to realize
that she has her choice.

00:58:01.360 --> 00:58:03.880
She can either stay in the past
with her family

00:58:03.880 --> 00:58:06.360
or she can move into the present
toward her new family,

00:58:06.360 --> 00:58:07.400
Geralt and Yen.

00:58:07.400 --> 00:58:08.800
Obviously that's what she does,

00:58:08.800 --> 00:58:10.080
and to demonstrate that,

00:58:10.080 --> 00:58:13.200
her family starts disappearing
into dust

00:58:13.200 --> 00:58:17.640
and going back to their form,
you know, which is dead.

00:58:17.640 --> 00:58:19.080
-They were dead.
-Yeah.

00:58:19.080 --> 00:58:22.800
Did you notice that,
in the final shot,

00:58:22.800 --> 00:58:25.880
Pavetta turns to dust
and disappears

00:58:25.880 --> 00:58:27.240
but Duny does not.

00:58:27.240 --> 00:58:28.280
Does not, he didn't.

00:58:28.280 --> 00:58:30.400
Yes, yes, I did notice that!

00:58:30.400 --> 00:58:33.200
That is because he is not dead.

00:58:33.200 --> 00:58:36.560
Wow, and so, that's,
I mean, it's so wonderful.

00:58:36.560 --> 00:58:37.600
It's so wonderful!

00:58:37.600 --> 00:58:38.800
Ah, I love this show so much.

00:58:38.800 --> 00:58:40.960
Okay, speaking of looking ahead,

00:58:40.960 --> 00:58:43.680
this season, we also dipped
a lot into the history

00:58:43.680 --> 00:58:45.440
of the Continent
with the Monoliths

00:58:45.440 --> 00:58:46.920
and the Conjunction
of the Spheres.

00:58:46.920 --> 00:58:49.160
Since we're getting the prequel,
Blood Origin,

00:58:49.160 --> 00:58:51.880
before Season 3,
how do you plant the seeds

00:58:51.880 --> 00:58:53.600
for the new series
in Season 2?

00:58:53.600 --> 00:58:54.920
Do we have seeds, Easter eggs?

00:58:54.920 --> 00:58:56.360
You absolutely have Easter eggs.

00:58:56.360 --> 00:58:58.840
So, one of our goals
has been to understand

00:58:58.840 --> 00:59:01.320
how this Continent came to be,
and it's one of the reasons,

00:59:01.320 --> 00:59:03.200
actually,
that we chose to make Istredd

00:59:03.200 --> 00:59:05.360
a bigger character
than he is in the books.

00:59:05.360 --> 00:59:07.480
We love the story,
Shard of Ice, in the books.

00:59:07.480 --> 00:59:10.920
We love that eventual
love triangle over Yennefer,

00:59:10.920 --> 00:59:12.520
between Istredd and Geralt,

00:59:12.520 --> 00:59:14.280
but as soon as we introduced
Istredd,

00:59:14.280 --> 00:59:17.040
Royce Pierreson was
so delightful in this role,

00:59:17.040 --> 00:59:20.800
and we realized that we had
a character that we could use

00:59:20.800 --> 00:59:22.800
to explore the history
of the Continent

00:59:22.800 --> 00:59:24.800
'cause he is a scholar,
he is a historian,

00:59:24.800 --> 00:59:27.800
he's a researcher,
and it was really helpful

00:59:27.800 --> 00:59:30.680
to put a voice to the things
that we really needed

00:59:30.680 --> 00:59:33.080
our audience to understand,
like what are these Monoliths,

00:59:33.080 --> 00:59:34.880
why are they here,
what are they doing?

00:59:34.880 --> 00:59:37.520
Obviously he and Geralt have
a sort of in-depth conversation

00:59:37.520 --> 00:59:41.080
about them this season
revealing that there is

00:59:41.080 --> 00:59:43.640
much more to the Conjunction
of the Spheres than we thought.

00:59:43.640 --> 00:59:46.120
That conversation
and a couple of other things

00:59:46.120 --> 00:59:49.760
throughout the season will all
be laid into Blood Origin.

00:59:49.760 --> 00:59:50.920
Wow, I cannot wait.

00:59:50.920 --> 00:59:52.640
I mean, the cast for that
is so incredible.

00:59:52.640 --> 00:59:53.920
When you mentioned
that one scene,

00:59:53.920 --> 00:59:55.800
I have to tell you,
one of my highlights

00:59:55.800 --> 00:59:57.960
of the whole--I know
this isn't about me, but...

00:59:57.960 --> 00:59:59.360
-No, tell me.
-One of the highlights

00:59:59.360 --> 01:00:02.440
of the whole season
was Geralt's reaction

01:00:02.440 --> 01:00:05.160
to hearing that he loved--

01:00:05.160 --> 01:00:08.560
I have never seen an actor
do so much with so little.

01:00:08.560 --> 01:00:10.200
There are so many
beautiful moments,

01:00:10.200 --> 01:00:11.680
but that one, oh my gosh.

01:00:11.680 --> 01:00:13.520
I just couldn't--
I couldn't deal.

01:00:13.520 --> 01:00:15.400
And to me,
it's like the two men,

01:00:15.400 --> 01:00:18.200
that moment where Istredd
calls her Yenna...

01:00:18.200 --> 01:00:20.280
-You know Yenna?
-Yenna...

01:00:20.280 --> 01:00:22.280
And they just face each other
and they both know

01:00:22.280 --> 01:00:25.040
in that moment that we have
our own names for her,

01:00:25.040 --> 01:00:28.240
we obviously have deep intimacy
and love for her and...

01:00:28.240 --> 01:00:29.840
Yeah, I don't mean--
I can't wait.

01:00:29.840 --> 01:00:31.280
I don't know
if it's gonna happen,

01:00:31.280 --> 01:00:33.800
but if the three of them meet,
oh boy!

01:00:33.800 --> 01:00:34.800
(laughter)

01:00:34.800 --> 01:00:36.520
Anyway, I could go on forever,

01:00:36.520 --> 01:00:38.880
but, Lauren, thank you so much
for letting me geek out

01:00:38.880 --> 01:00:40.440
with you over your show.

01:00:40.440 --> 01:00:42.800
As a fan, I can't wait
to see what comes

01:00:42.800 --> 01:00:44.080
for the Witcher franchise.

01:00:44.080 --> 01:00:45.800
Thank you so much,
this is so much fun,

01:00:45.800 --> 01:00:47.120
and I love talking
about the finale.

01:00:47.120 --> 01:00:48.440
I haven't been able to at all.

01:00:48.440 --> 01:00:50.400
It must be a little bit
of relief, yeah, I know.

01:00:50.400 --> 01:00:52.080
It's really fun for fans
to see this,

01:00:52.080 --> 01:00:54.160
but I just really urge everyone,
watch it again

01:00:54.160 --> 01:00:56.640
because not only are
the Easter eggs there

01:00:56.640 --> 01:00:58.800
that Lauren just mentioned,
there are a ton more.

01:00:58.800 --> 01:01:00.600
I mean, just even
with the graphics

01:01:00.600 --> 01:01:03.760
coming in for the beginning
of each episode being different

01:01:03.760 --> 01:01:05.880
and thematically tied
to the episode,

01:01:05.880 --> 01:01:07.760
it's just fantastic,
it really is.

01:01:07.760 --> 01:01:09.880
So, yay, thank you so much.

01:01:09.880 --> 01:01:11.280
Thank you!

01:01:11.280 --> 01:01:14.600
Join us in 2022
for more episodes of Unlocked

01:01:14.600 --> 01:01:17.640
